	abstract = {To investigate the impact of incorporating Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) methodologies into fraud detection Machine Learning (ML) models, this study employs Experimental research to perform a comprehensive analysis of the integration of SHAP and LIME explainability methods into ML models for fraud detection using Ethereum dataset which is comprised of 9,840 transactions, assessing performance before and after the application of XAI methods on Random Forest (RF) and XGBoost. The two ML models attained an accuracy of 98%. The experiment revealed that the incorporation of XAI into ML models does not directly influence their performance; however, it results in heightened CPU overhead, increased latency, and diminished throughput. Specifically, the RF model utilizing LIME exhibited a latency of 14.67 seconds and 145.2511 seconds, for experiments 1 and 2, respectively, alongside the lowest throughput recorded of 6.76 and 5.37 transactions processed, with average CPU of Incorporating lime with XGBoost peaking at 99.7% and 99.9% for experiment 1 and 2 respectively. These findings elucidate the previously underexamined performance trade-offs associated with the integration of XAI techniques into fraud detection models. © 2026 The Authors.},
