	affiliations = {School of Computer Science and Technology, Shandong University of Technology, Shandong, Dongying, China},
	abstract = {This study presents an intelligent framework for predicting actual compensation amounts in logistics claims management, addressing the critical challenge of balancing customer satisfaction with cost control. We develop a hierarchical stacking ensemble that synthesizes predictions from eight heterogeneous base learners—including Random Forest, XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost, KNN, SVM, GPR, and DNN—through meta-learning via linear regression and gradient boosting. Our model achieves robust generalization across training, validation, and test sets, with prediction errors concentrated at compensation threshold boundaries. To enhance transparency, we implement SHAP value decomposition revealing that claim amount disparity, original claim amount, and package declared value dominate settlement decisions. SHAP dependence analysis with polynomial regression identifies critical thresholds in feature-compensation relationships, enabling rule-based policy optimization. Experimental results demonstrate superior predictive accuracy while providing actionable insights for automated claim, fraud detection, and personalized service recommendations, establishing a trustworthy AI solution for logistics risk management. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
