	abstract = {Credit card fraud detection requires highly accurate and interpretable models to minimize financial losses and build trust in automated systems. Although machine learning models achieve high predictive accuracy, their deployment is often hindered by class imbalance, computational efficiency, and interpretability challenges. Many existing studies overlook precision–recall performance under extreme imbalance, computational burden, and explanation reliability. This study addresses these challenges using the PaySim dataset, which simulates mobile money transactions with extreme class imbalance. The research investigates four core dimensions: SMOTE for imbalance mitigation, explainable AI (XAI) methods using SHAP and LIME for interpretability, learning-curve analysis for data sufficiency insights, and computational cost assessment reflecting real-time system constraints. Multiple advanced ensemble models were developed and evaluated using 5-fold cross-validation, including AdaBoostClassifier with RandomForestClassifier and a StackingClassifier combining ExtraTrees, AdaBoost variants, and XGBoost. A dual-strategy framework is proposed: StackingClassifier as the primary model when computational resources are available, and AdaBoostClassifier with RandomForest as an efficient alternative for resource-constrained environments. Both models were evaluated under two operational scenarios: post-transaction and real-time fraud detection. In post-transaction scenarios, the StackingClassifier achieved 99.99% accuracy, 0.9999 F1 score, 0.9999 precision, 1.0 recall, and 0.9999 AUC, while AdaBoostClassifier achieved 99.99% accuracy, 0.9999 F1 score, 0.9998 precision, 1.0 recall, and 1.0 AUC. In real-time scenarios (excluding balance-related features unavailable during live processing), both maintained superior performance despite feature reduction. Comprehensive analysis on AdaBoostClassifier with RandomForest demonstrated consistent, interpretable explanations and computational efficiency. The study offers a resource-aware framework balancing accuracy, efficiency, and interpretability across different operational contexts. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
	author_keywords = {Deep learning; Ensemble learning; Fraud detection; Machine learning; SMOTE; XAI},
	keywords = {Adaptive boosting; Automation; Balancing; Computational efficiency; Crime; Data mining; Electronic money; Interactive computer systems; Learning systems; Losses; Real time systems; Class imbalance; Deep learning; Ensemble learning; F1 scores; Fraud detection; Interpretability; Machine-learning; Real- time; SMOTE; XAI; Deep learning},
