	abstract = {Credit card fraud is one of the foremost issues when it comes to transactions online, with countries suffering millions in losses.Traditional solutions to tackling fraud typically rely on static rules and linear models which cannot keep up with the variety and ever-evolving nature of fraud.To combat this, we propose a hybrid model combining Autoencoders feature extraction and Random Forest classification to detect fraudulent credit cards transactions. In this work, the Autoencoder (AE) is first trained to detect anomalies in the normal structure of the transaction, and anything falling outside of that profile is determined to be fraud. “Useful” later features for detection of abnormal activity from normal transactions are extracted and summed with data from the original dataset as well which is then classified by Random Forest for increased robustness. Very high accuracy and ROC-AUC prove that this AE + RF hybrid is successful at differentiating fraudulent and legitimate transactions even in highly skewed datasets. © 2026 IEEE.},
