	title = {A Scalable Explainable AI Framework for Phishing Attack Detection with Interpretable Feature Insights with Machine Learning Based Ensemble Models},
	year = {2025},
	journal = {IEEE International Conference on Innovate for Humanitarian: Tech Solutions for Global Challenge, ICIH 2025},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/ICIH67754.2025.11608371},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046411022?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Lnct Collage, Cse Department, Bhopal, India; SISTec, Cse Department, Gandhinagar, Bhopal, India},
	abstract = {Phishing is a kind of online fraud when the attacker impersonates a trustworthy company to fool the victim into disclosing personal information. It requires detection technologies that are both accurate and easy to understand since it is still one of the most common and harmful cybersecurity threats. Predicting phishing incidents using data on the underlying website URLs is a strong suit of ML algorithms, making them ideal for this task. Applying ML to the problem of phishing URL detection, with an emphasis on how feature selection and model interpretability greatly affect recovery rates. This research presents an AI framework for detecting phishing attacks that is both scalable and explicable. The approach makes use of ensemble-based ML models, such as Stacking Classifiers, Bagging, and CatBoost. The method exploits the Phishing Website dataset, combining exploratory data analysis, mutual information-based feature selection and MinMax scaling and SMOTE Tomek as a data preprocessing operation to address the issue of class imbalance. The models were also trained and tested with and without feature selection, but the Stacking Classifier displayed the best performance, reaching a 96.54 percent accuracy, 98 percent recall, and 96.65 percent F1 score. The effectiveness and interpretability of the models were evaluated using confusion matrices and ROC curves and SHAP graphs. The proposed method shows competitive and, in some situations, superior performance than other existing models such as LightGBM, XGBoost, and SVM using a comparative analysis. The study's outcomes emphasize that effective feature selection and model explainability are crucial aspects of enhancing phishing detection systems, paving the way for more flexible and productive countermeasures against adaptable cyber threats. The framework proves to be both accurate and interpretable, making it well-suited for real-world cybersecurity applications. © 2025 IEEE.},
