	abstract = {The fast development of generative artificial intelligence has enabled highly realistic synthetic facial imaging, raising significant concerns about identity fraud, deception, and digital security. Traditional approaches don't provide a complete understanding of the interconnected evolution of deepfakes; instead, they concentrate on either deepfake creation or detection independently. This paper suggests a single generative AI system that addresses both the creation and detection of deepfake images by carefully integrating four distinct deep learning architectures. The system uses diffusion models and generative adversarial networks to create high-fidelity synthetic facial images and variational autoencoders and vision transformers to recognize and classify deepfakes with reliability. The framework performs exceptionally well in terms of both generation quality and detection accuracy metrics after being trained and assessed on the Kaggle Deepfake and Real Dataset, which consists of real and artificially created facial images. Grad-CAM visualization and attention mapping are two explainable AI approaches that the system uses to improve the interpretability and transparency of the model. Real-time image production and verification capabilities are made possible by an intuitive Streamlit interface, which supports both forensic applications and scientific advancement. In digital media forensics, this integrated approach shows that unified multi-model systems can successfully close the gap between deepfake development and detection while preserving transparency, dependability, and usefulness. © 2026 IEEE.},
