	abstract = {The rapid expansion of social media platforms has increased the availability of publicly accessible personal data while simultaneously facilitating the proliferation of fraudulent or automated accounts that exploit this data for malicious purposes, such as phishing, identity theft, spam dissemination, and misinformation campaigns, creating risks for user security and trust. Detecting such accounts remains challenging due to the limited availability of labeled data and the rapidly evolving strategies used by malicious actors. This study proposes a framework for identifying suspicious Instagram accounts through the integration of data mining, social network analysis, and machine learning techniques. Publicly available Instagram data were collected using an automated crawling process that retrieves profile metadata, textual information, and follower relationships. From this dataset, a set of behavioral and structural network features was engineered to capture different dimensions of user activity. The detection approach combines unsupervised anomaly detection methods with supervised learning models, while incorporating explainable machine learning techniques to interpret model decisions. In particular, Shapley-based explanations are used to analyze how different features influence predictions and to provide insights into the behavioral and structural characteristics associated with fake accounts. Experimental results show that structural network properties and behavioral indicators jointly play a key role in identifying suspicious accounts. The study highlights the importance of combining graph-based features with behavioral signals and emphasizes the value of explainable artificial intelligence methods for improving transparency and interpretability in social media fraud detection. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.},
