	abstract = {Blockchain financial systems such as Bitcoin are increasingly vulnerable to sophisticated illicit activities, yet existing anomaly detection approaches remain limited by reliance on labeled data and static feature sets. This study explores an unsupervised learning framework based on a Variational Autoencoder ensemble augmented with LSTM layers to capture both complex transactional patterns and temporal dependencies. By training solely on raw transaction attributes including amount, fee, gas price, mining pool, currency, frequency, and z-scores the model learns a compact representation of normal behavior and assigns reconstruction-error–based anomaly scores. Our empirical analysis demonstrates that the proposed Autoencoder-LSTM achieves superior performance, reducing mean squared error to 0.0025 an order of magnitude improvement over traditional clustering and one-class baselines while maintaining high cluster cohesion (Silhouette ≈0.58). Complementary statistical tests, permutation-based feature importance, and SHAP explainability analyses reveal that fee-related features and gas prices are the primary drivers of anomaly decisions. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd},
