	abstract = {Financial fraud in distributed aerospace ecosystems poses escalating risks to operational safety and regulatory compliance. Existing federated learning (FL) approaches to fraud detection assume IID data across clients and provide minimal decision transparency. This paper proposes FL-CoRT, a privacy-preserving framework combining Federated Averaging (FedAvg) under non-IID conditions with a Chain of Recursive Thought explainability module. Evaluated on the IEEE-CIS dataset across five heterogeneous clients partitioned via Dirichlet distribution (α = 5.0), the global model achieves AUC-ROC of 0.917 and AUPRC of 0.616, retaining 99.7% of centralised performance while preserving data privacy. The CoRT module produces stable (Jaccard = 0. 985) and faithful (7. 7 × fidelity ratio) multi-step explanations bounded at recursion depth d ≤ 3, providing audit-ready rationales surpassing static SHAP/LIME. To the best of our knowledge, FL-CoRT is a framework that simultaneously addresses non-IID heterogeneity and structured multi-step explainability in federated fraud detection. © 2026 IEEE.},
