	affiliations = {University of Florida, Computer & Information Science & Engineering, Gainesville, FL, United States; University of Florida, Gainesville, FL, United States},
	abstract = {Financial institutions rely on tree-ensemble fraud filters, but their opacity impedes trust and regulatory review. Stakeholders require transparent reasoning to validate alerts, debug model behavior, and ensure fairness. We address this by benchmarking eight explainability techniques on a 100 -tree custom Random Forest trained on 100 000 anonymized transactions. The benchmark spans three levels of analysis: global importance (MDI, Permutation Importance, TreeSHAP), marginaleffect (PDP & ICE), and local attribution (LIME, KernelSHAP, TreeInterpreter), supplemented by an FCI-Max causal discovery graph (via rCausalMGM) to map potential causal and confounding relationships. The baseline model attains 0.984 accuracy and a 0.883 F1-score. Our findings show significant performance disparities: model-specific TreeSHAP requires only 0.86 s versus the model-agnostic Permutation Importance's 377 s for a global overview. Feature-rank agreement is modest (Kendall τ=0.244 for MDI-SHAP), underscoring that no single metric captures the full picture. Crucially, ICE curves expose non-monotonic risk escalations invisible to global importance bars, while local explainers show variable faithfulness and sparsity. We propose a multi-level framework combining fast global scores with detailed effect-level and case-level views to support more transparent and actionable fraud screening.  © 2025 IEEE.},
