	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/SysCon66367.2026.11503480},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105040740458?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {VA, United States; NC, United States; TX, United States},
	abstract = {Healthcare providers have begun to use artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) models to help detect and prevent fraud, waste and abuse in their claims processing systems. Healthcare claims processing systems process large amounts of high dimensional data including claim dollar amount, diagnosis codes, procedure patterns, and time series of data from provider behavior. The use of AI for detecting healthcare fraud has been shown to improve both the efficiency of detecting fraud and increase the rate of detection; however, this technology may introduce some unaddressed ethics risks including: discrimination or bias in algorithms, lack of transparency in decision making, automated enforcement actions without human oversight, misuse of probabilistic outputs for fraud detection, and disclosure of confidential patient health information.Regulatory agencies will consider ethical failure to be an unethical claim denial based upon no justifiable reason, discriminatory review of certain types of providers, loss of public trust in providers and patients, and potential violation of regulatory provisions such as HIPAA and CMS Program Integrity Guidelines. Traditional model validations typically evaluate predictive performance of ML/AI models and do not provide a method to systematically assess, quantify, or mitigate ethics risks before deployment.The authors propose an Ethical Risk Assessment Framework (ERAF), which is designed to integrate ethics evaluation into the model validation lifecycle of AI/ML driven healthcare fraud detection systems. The ERAF uses a structured approach to assess four areas of ethics risk: data governance, fairness, explainability, and operational governance, as well as robustness of the system. By using simulated healthcare claims data and test cases to simulate different scenarios, the authors demonstrate that the ERAF can reduce the measurable level of ethics risk associated with AI/ML driven healthcare fraud detection systems and maintain predictive performance. The authors believe that their proposed approach will enable healthcare organizations to deploy AI/ML systems responsibly in high stakes fraud detection environments with the capability to audit their deployments to meet governance needs. The framework was demonstrated using a synthetic dataset created with the goal of mimicking the most common features of healthcare claims data, in order to provide an initial feasibility assessment, as opposed to a full-scale production deployment study.  © 2026 IEEE.},
