	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105049050847?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Astana It University, School of Digital Public Administration, Astana, Kazakhstan; Almaty Technological University, Department of Accounting and Finance, Almaty, Kazakhstan; National Institute of Intellectual Property Qazpatent, Department of Development and International Cooperation, Astana, Kazakhstan; Institute of Economics and Project Management, Astana, Kazakhstan},
	abstract = {This paper examines how data analytics can improve financial monitoring and risk control in banking, with a specific empirical orientation toward the Kazakhstan banking sector. The study responds to a practical limitation in many risk-control environments: banks and supervisory bodies increasingly possess high-frequency transaction and portfolio data, but monitoring still relies heavily on static thresholds, fragmented indicators, and delayed portfolio-quality reports. We propose a risk-oriented analytics pipeline that combines public aggregate banking indicators with a synthetic transaction-level dataset calibrated to Kazakhstan's macro-financial conditions. The empirical section evaluates four approaches: a rule-based monitoring baseline, weighted logistic regression, a tree-feature logistic model, and a hybrid monitoring score that combines supervised default/suspicion probabilities with behavioral anomaly signals. On the 2024 holdout sample of 3,000 observations, the hybrid score achieves the highest ROC-AUC of 0.915 and F1-score of 0.544 at an operational alert threshold, while reducing false alerts compared with the rule-based baseline. The findings suggest that even a transparent, audit-oriented analytical design can materially improve early-warning capacity without relying on inaccessible proprietary bank data. The paper contributes a reproducible structure for emerging-market banking analytics, where data confidentiality, explainability, and supervisory usability are as important as predictive accuracy. © 2026 IEEE.},
