	abstract = {The increase which has been witnessed in the digital commerce has triggered the corresponding increase in credit card frauds thus triggering the establishment of advanced detecting mechanism which has the capabilities of defending either an individual or organization. In our work, we explore a range of classical machine learning paradigms Logistic Regression, Decision Trees, Random Forest, Support Vector Machines, Neural Networks and newer deep learning architectures LSTM and BiLSTM to detect fraud. Similarly, to reverse the class skewing that is part of the nature of most card related data, Synthetic Minority Oversampling Technique (SMOTE) is adopted and PCA is applied in the improvement of feature quality and reduction of dimensionality. The accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, and the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC-ROC) are measured as empirical performance. Based on these results, it can be concluded that BiLSTM retains high accuracy and precision compared to standard algorithms and has a significant reduction in the volume of false positives on various datasets. Further, the findings also bring out various operational issues as well as desirable future aspects, including the integration into rule-based strategies, explainable AI (XAI), security improvements on a blockchain, federated learning, and adaptive reinforcement learning, in order to develop effective and real-time fraud detection systems.  © 2025 IEEE.},
