	affiliations = {Pan-Atlantic University, Lagos, Nigeria},
	abstract = {As digital solutions grow and become indispensable to our everyday lives, personal and business information has also become omnipresent. Some of this information includes payment and financial data, which is increasingly vulnerable to exposure to fraudsters. However, many financial institutions, practitioners, and researchers have, over the years, tried to tighten their cybersecurity measures to mitigate and potentially reduce the number of fraudulent transactions. Indeed, they have adopted various approaches, including rule-based, human-in-the-loop, and more recently, machine-learning and deep-learning-based approaches. Nevertheless, financial analysts have seen many of these models as 'black-box' models whose inner workings are not transparent; thus, there is still some gap regarding exploring more explainable, interpretable, resilient, distributed, and real-time fraud-detection systems to promptly report any suspicious transaction in order to enable the financial analysts to act quickly. In this work, a combination of explainable and hybrid machine-learning approaches, along with a data-engineering pipeline, was adopted to immediately report any fraudulent transaction in real-time (in less than a second) before the transaction is committed. With an average F1-score of 98% and an AUC of 96%, the adopted explainable, interpretable, and real-time approach can inspire microfinance and other financial institutions to tighten their defenses. © 2025 IEEE.},
