	affiliations = {Malla Reddy Engineering College (Autonomous) Main Campus, Hyderabad, India; B V Raju Institute of Technology, Hyderabad, India; Vignan Institute of and Information Technology (VIIT), Duvvada JNTU, GV, India; Sree Vahini Institute of Science and STechnology(A), N.T.R district, Tiruvuru, India; School of Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence, SR University, Warangal, India},
	abstract = {Detecting credit card fraud is a major challenge in the financial industry, primarily because fraudulent transactions are rare compared to legitimate ones, and there’s a growing need for AI systems to be explainable and trustworthy. This study introduces a practical and effective machine learning approach to address these issues. We used the Synthetic Minority Oversampling Technique (SMOTE) to handle the imbalance between fraudulent and non-fraudulent transactions and SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) to make the model’s predictions more transparent and understandable. Our approach uses CatBoost, a machine learning algorithm tailored for handling categorical data, and incorporates a preprocessing step to standardize numerical features. When tested on a credit card fraud dataset, the model performed exceptionally well, achieving an accuracy of 99.97% and a ROC AUC score of 99.99%. The detailed classification report showed equally strong results for detecting both fraud and non-fraud cases, with precision, recall, and F1-scores all at 1.00. To ensure the system isn’t just accurate but also interpretable, we used SHAP analysis to explain the influence of different features on the model’s decisions. This transparency provides valuable insights that can guide more effective fraud prevention strategies. The results highlight the potential of this framework to achieve near-perfect fraud detection while remaining explainable and user-friendly, making it a strong candidate for real-world applications in the financial sector. © 2026 Author(s).},
