	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045439007?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Berkeley Heights, NJ, United States; TCS, Irving, United States},
	abstract = {Modern banking systems are facing a serious threat from financial fraud due to large-scale transactions, sophisticated attack patterns and rigorous, but demanding regulations. Traditional detection models offering high accuracy, but poor explainability to auditors and risk managers, are sometimes used. This research presents an interpretable machine learning approach to financial fraud detection in banks via Explainable Artificial Intelligence. The approach combines solid data preprocessing, addressing class imbalance through adaptive resampling and a combined ensemble of gradient boosting and rule-based models to detect fraudulent transactions. Explainability is achieved using global and local explanation layers via feature attribution, surrogate reasoning and counterfactual analysis, allowing practitioners to explain model predictions and transaction risk factors. A trust-calibration system prioritises alerts for further investigation based on confidence and explainability stability. The proposed method is validated on banking transaction data sets using accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, AUC and false alarm rate. Experimentation results suggest a better performance in detecting fraud, fewer false alarms, and better explainability compared to conventional black-box models, enabling compliant, robust and user-friendly banking fraud analytics.  © 2026 IEEE.},
