	title = {Cognitive-Adaptive AI for Enterprise Account Management: A Validated Six-Pillar Framework},
	year = {2026},
	journal = {ISADES 2026 - 2nd International Symposium on AI-Driven Engineering Systems, Proceedings},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/ISADES69945.2026.11608221},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046094698?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Bayan University, Business Administration Department, Kurdistan, Erbil, Iraq; University of Samarra, Samarra, Iraq; AL-Ma'moon University, Department of accounting, Baghdad, Iraq; Al-Esraa Universit, Department of Accounting, Baghdad, Iraq; Al-Hikma University College, Baghdad, Iraq},
	abstract = {Account management plays a critical role in customer retention, growth, and long-term business profitability. Traditional account management approaches often struggle with limited customer insights, delayed response to customer needs, and scalability challenges that prevent personalized engagement across large customer bases. This paper presents the Cognitive-Adaptive AI Account Management (CAAM) framework, an intelligent platform designed to enhance customer relationship management through adaptive AI-driven decision-making. The framework integrates six core components, including behavioral customer understanding, dynamic personalization, predictive risk detection, omnichannel interaction management, explainable AI governance, and continuous learning mechanisms. The proposed system introduces three key innovations: behavioral readiness modeling for customer engagement, context-aware churn prediction that adapts to market conditions, and privacypreserving cross-organization learning across non-competing firms. The framework is evaluated through an extensive review of recent research and multiple real-world case studies across banking, telecommunications, insurance, and retail sectors. Experimental findings demonstrate significant improvements in customer retention, upselling performance, fraud reduction, and customer satisfaction compared to traditional account management methods. The study also identifies practical deployment challenges related to fairness, governance, organizational adaptation, and regulatory compliance. The results highlight the transformative potential of AI-driven account management systems and provide future directions for autonomous customer management, emotion-aware AI, digital customer twins, and next-generation intelligent business ecosystems. The study adopts a mixed-method design combining a systematic literature review of 47 studies (2019-2025), conceptual framework specification, and controlled empirical evaluation across 12 enterprise deployments totalling 8.58 million accounts, with all performance claims benchmarked against concurrent rule-based control groups using A/Bcontrolled protocols over 12-month observation windows.  © 2026 IEEE.},
