	affiliations = {SCSET, Bennett University, Greater Noida, India},
	abstract = {As digital payments become more prevalent across the globe, attacks have become more sophisticated, leading to an increase in financial fraud. Consequently, systems must be defended using forward-thinking methods to stay ahead of this evolving landscape. Traditional methods of fraud detection involve the use of known rules, but the inflexibility of these systems often causes them to fail in detecting new schemes. This study aims to address these limitations through machine learning(ML) by dynamically exploring transaction data to reveal the underlying patterns of fraud. Research includes deep learning models such as the Conversion Neural Network(CNN),Artificial Neural Network(ANN), Support Vector Machine (SVM) etc., which are increasingly used in dealing with these complex fraud scenarios. Model performance is improved by implementing feature engineering techniques such as Label Encoding, SMOTE, and PCA. A major finding of our work is that adaptive ML systems which integrate multiple models and human feedback-are effective at reducing false positives while increasing the detection of fraudulent activities. The paper concludes with a call to es-tablish collaborative frameworks involving financial institutions, policymakers, and cybersecurity professionals to develop ethical, transparent, and scalable fraud mitigation strategies. © 2025 IEEE.},
