	year = {2026},
	journal = {2026 IEEE 2nd International Conference on Quantum Photonics, Artificial Intelligence and Networking, QPAIN 2026},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/QPAIN69676.2026.11546654},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105042842925?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Department of Mechanical and Manufacturing Engineering, Faculty of Engineering and Build Environments (FKAB), University Kebangsaan Malaysia, UKM Bangi, Selangor, 43600, Malaysia; International Islamic University, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Chattogram, Chittagong, Bangladesh; Premier University, Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering (EEE), Bangladesh},
	abstract = {Detection of financial fraud has now emerged as a critical issue in the banking system of the modern world as the number of digital transactions is growing, as well as their complexity. Current methods of detecting fraud are usually based on completely labeled data, which cannot be easily found in practice and restricts the model to adapt to new types of fraud. In response to this gap, the current paper suggests a hybrid framework of detecting fraud that combines the unsupervised anomaly detection and the supervised pseudo-labeled data classification framework. The framework is tested on a pseudo-labeled bank transaction dataset that is synthesized out of actual transactional features, in which the anomaly detection models are initially used to point towards suspicious transactions and then changed into fraud labels that are subjected to supervised learning. The imbalance-aware metrics (accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, and ROC-AUC) are used to measure the model performance. By experiment, we have shown that Support Vector Machines work well with a high accuracy of 97% with a maximum ROC-AUC of 0.97, and low false-negative rates. The further explanation of the AI analysis provided by SHAP shows that loginattempts, accountbalance, and transactionamount have the biggest impact as the predictors of fraud. The suggested framework is an effective and explainable framework in detecting fraud in labelscarce settings that provide a practical understanding towards real-world banking. Extended work Future work will build on this framework with real-time streaming transaction data to detect frauds in real-time. © 2026 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Banking Transactions; Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI); Fraud Detection; Hybrid Machine Learning; Isolation Forest; Support Vector Machine; Unsupervised Model},
