	affiliations = {University of Petra, Department of Computer Science, Amman, Jordan; Al-Bayan University, College of Engineering, Baghdad, Iraq; Taibah University, College of Computer Science and Engineering, Medina, Saudi Arabia},
	abstract = {Energy theft and other non-technical losses impose substantial financial and operational burdens on utility providers. Conventional monitoring pipelines struggle to identify increasingly sophisticated fraud patterns in smart meter telemetry, particularly under severe class imbalance and time-varying consumption behaviour. This paper proposes an integrated IoT-AI framework for near-real-time anomaly detection across smart utility infrastructures. The architecture is organised into four functional layers: IoT-enabled data acquisition, intelligent pre-processing with adaptive feature extraction, parallel machine learning analytics, and operator-orientated decision support. The detection pipeline integrates three complementary models. Evaluation is conducted using the State Grid Corporation of China (SGCC) benchmark, which includes 42,372 consumers and 1,036 daily measurements per consumer. To avoid optimistic reporting under extreme imbalance, classification performance is reported on a stratified test subset that preserves all theft cases and samples normal consumers. On this subset, the framework achieves 94.3% accuracy, 92.8% precision, 93.5% recall, and a 93.1% F1-score, while maintaining average alert latency below 2.5 seconds on the specified testbed. Explainable AI is incorporated through SHAP-based attribution to provide operator-facing rationales that support auditability and inspection prioritisation. © 2026 IEEE.},
