	abstract = {The growing complexity of global supply networks has made the risks of fraud, data tampering and inefficiencies even more pronounced, which is a major challenge to transparency and trust. Traditional centralized or rule-based systems are known to be static, slow and incapable of adapting to changing patterns of fraud. This study helps to overcome these challenges by proposing an Autonomous Intelligence for Monitoring Supply Networks (AIM-SN) framework - a multi-agent AI ecosystem that is aimed at continuous monitoring and decentralized mitigation of fraud. The model combines the knowledge graph reasoning, deep learning and federated reinforcement learning to facilitate the self-learning, explainable and privacy-preserving decision-making. Using a simulated multimodal data set that included ERP transactions, IoT logistics data and blockchain traceability data, AIM-SN was able to provide 94% accuracy in fraud detection, an F1-score of 92.5% and a Trust Consistency Index (TCI) of 0.92, in comparison to centralized ML, blockchain-only and rule-based systems. The results show AIM-SN's potential for revolutionizing supply chain governance using autonomous, transparent, and adaptive intelligence to build a foundation for implementation in real-world settings for cross-organizational fraud management.  © 2026 IEEE.},
