	abstract = {The greater level of complexity of financial fraud schemes and the increasing amount of financial transactions in online settings have complicated financial fraud detection. Although Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML) models have demonstrated impressive accuracy in their ability to detect-anomalies and forecast fraud, these models are inherently black-box, which makes them highly questionable in terms of transparency, accountability, and trust. The Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) has become a paramount paradigm to overcome all these problems and offer the possibility of making the process of decision-making transparent and interpretable without sacrificing predictive performance. The paper investigates the utilization of XAI methods within financial fraud detection systems, highlighting how these approaches contribute to increasing the interpretability and regulatory compliance as well as the confidence of stakeholders of the applied models. The paper has systematically reviewed the explainability of methods and their applications in predicting accounting outcomes to support the use of explainability as a tool to educate and justify decision-making by auditors, regulators, and financial institutions. In addition, the work shows that the ability to close the divide between predictive performance and interpretability enhances trust on the part of customers and institutions as well as curbs risks of bias and unfair results. Finally, the study highlights the importance of XAI-based fraud detection systems as a viable solution, sustainability-wise, which not only enhances the precision but also enables transparency, accountability, and ethical AI usage by the financial industry. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2027.},
