	abstract = {The growing prevalence of credit card fraud presents a critical risk to financial security, highlighting the need for intelligent and automated systems capable of detecting and resolving cyberattacks. This review article presents recent studies on credit card fraud detection (CCFD), with a focus on Machine Learning (ML), Deep Learning (DL), and hybrid methods. ML algorithms, including decision trees (DT), support vector machines (SVM), and random forests (RF), are commonly used to analyze transactions and detect risks. Moreover, DL models, including convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and recurrent neural networks (RNNs), have proven more effective in detecting sophisticated fraud operations because they can model complex relationships in large datasets. Hybrid methods combining the strengths of both paradigms are gaining popularity, with higher detection rates and lower false positives. The study systematically examines the advantages and disadvantages of each method, comparing benchmarking criteria, real-world test sets, and practical deployment issues. The most relevant trends and open research areas are identified, such as explainable AI and real-Time fraud detection. Overall, this review aims to educate researchers and practitioners through a thorough understanding of the evolution of credit card cyberattack detection methods.  © 2025 IEEE.},
