	abstract = {AI-driven fraud detection identifies suspicious patterns and anomalies in financial transactions, improving security by automating the detection process. It reduces the need for manual intervention by enabling organizations to react swiftly to possible threats. False positives, which mark legitimate transactions as fraudulent, could be produced by the system. It also requires regular updates to stay effective against evolving fraud tactics. To overcome this problem, in this manuscript AI-Driven Fraud Detection and risk management in business processes: A cross-industry study (AIDFD-GGNN) is proposed. At first, the input data are gathered from Fraudulent Transaction Detection Dataset. The data are pre-processed using Adaptive Two-Stage Unscented Kalman Filter (ATUKF), which is used missing data and outlier removal the input data. Then the data are fed into Human Evolutionary Optimization Algorithm (HEOA) for feature selection. The selected features are provided to Gegenbauer Graph Neural Networks (GGNN) for detect the fraud and classify such as legitimate transaction and fraudulent transaction. The proposed technique is implemented in Python, and the efficacy of the AIDFD-GGNN technique was assessed using various performance measures, like accuracy, recall, precision, and the Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) curve. The performance of the AIDFD-GGNN method achieved 99.54% higher Accuracy; 99.14%higher Precision; 99.16% higher Recall when analysed through existing techniques such as Transparency and Privacy: The Role of Explainable AI and Federated Learning in Financial Fraud Detection (TPAI-FFD-ML), Enhancing Medicare Fraud Detection with a CNN-Transformer-XGBoost Framework and Explainable AI (MFD-EAI-CNN) and Explainable Artificial Intelligence and Causal Inference based ATM Fraud Detection (ATM-FD-GAN), respectively. © 2025 IEEE.},
