	abstract = {Personal finance applications have long confined themselves to historical summaries, leaving the decisions users actually face unsupported by the machine learning models capable of informing them: whether to trust a transaction, how much to expect to spend next month and what budget to commit to. SmartSpend is a unified AI-powered personal finance dashboard that closes this gap not by aggregating model outputs but by restructuring each one into a format aligned with a specific user decision. A calibrated risk tier surfaces the post-settlement risk signal for each transaction, supporting informed review of flagged activity rather than intervening at the point of authorization. A forecast with confidence intervals gives the user something concrete to plan against. A learned budget recommendation replaces the static heuristic with a figure the model has earned through temporal validation. The fraud detection module achieves AUC-ROC of 0.9921 and F1 of 0.7836 on a stratified sample of 100,000 transactions drawn from a publicly available dataset of 1.29 million records across 983 cardholders. The operating threshold is selected to limit false positive volume below the alert fatigue threshold. The LightGBM forecasting module achieves an 18.80% reduction in mean absolute error over the naive baseline under walk-forward temporal validation across 50 users and serves directly as the foundation for the budget recommendation engine. A systematic capability comparison across eight dimensions confirms that no existing consumer application or prior ML research satisfies more than two of these requirements simultaneously. The contribution is the integration architecture: a principled system that communicates uncertainty at every output, exposes its reasoning chain to non-technical users and calibrates operating thresholds for human trust rather than statistical optimality alone. © 2026 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Budget Recommendation; Dashboard; Explainable AI; Fraud Detection; Human-AI interaction; LightGBM; Personal Finance; Spending Forecasting},
