	abstract = {Social engineering attacks in real-time chat environments exploit psychological manipulation to extract sensitive information, posing significant risks to individuals and organizations. Traditional detection methods often struggle with high latency, limited contextual awareness, and poor interpretability, rendering them inadequate for dynamic conversational settings. This paper proposes a novel hybrid system integrating lightweight rule-based pattern matching with machine learning, leveraging DistilBERT embeddings and engineered features for accurate, low-latency detection of social engineering attacks. A rolling fraud score mechanism captures multi-turn conversational threats, while a non-recursive architecture ensures memory efficiency. The proposed method has been evaluated using a composite of real and semi-synthetic multi-turn chat datasets, the hybrid model achieved consistent message and conversation-level performance with 93% accuracy and low latency (<500ms), outperforming existing methods in both precision and real-time applicability. © 2026 IEEE.},
