	affiliations = {Cmr Institute of Technology, Bengaluru, India; Bits Pilani-Dubai Campus, United Arab Emirates},
	abstract = {As e-commerce continues to experience rapid growth, credit cards serve as the preferred method for financial payments. With the ease of access to credit cards, the withdrawal of funds through credit cards has exponentially increased. This also paves the way to serious fraud, resulting in losses to users and financial institutions. Data imbalance causes a major challenge in the identification of fraud. To address this, a framework is proposed that utilizes multiple machine learning algorithms, such as Logistic Regression, Naïve Bayes, K-Nearest Neighbors, Random Forest, AdaBoost, and Gradient Boosting, along with the Synthetic Minority Oversampling Technique (SMOTE) to balance the datasets. This enhances the capability of models to learn significant fraud patterns. Furthermore, the prediction can be interpreted with Explainable AI (XAI) algorithms like SHAP and LIME that provide insight into the most important elements and contribute to identifying fraudulent activity. Experimental assessment of a real-world Kaggle dataset shows that Gradient Boosting and Random Forest demonstrate the best performance. Together with data balancing through SMOTE and interpretability through XAI, the proposed system is effective and usable in real-life finance fraud prevention. © 2025 IEEE.},
