	affiliations = {Federal University SPbGUT, Infocommunication Systems Dep, Saint Petersburg, Russian Federation; AB Handshake, Telecom Fraud Prevention Dep, Miami, United States; Virginia Tech, Department of Mathematics, Blacksburg, United States},
	abstract = {This paper presents an overview of modern engineering and mathematical approaches to Fraud Management Systems (FMS) in telecommunication networks. It highlights the growing scale and sophistication of fraud in the transition from PSTN to 5G/6G, including interconnect fraud, CLI spoofing, SIMbox fraud, and False Answer Supervision (FAS). The architecture of typical FMS solutions is analyzed in the context of OSS/BSS integration and real-Time processing of signaling and xDR data. The paper reviews key mathematical models, including statistical techniques (Z-score, t-Test, chi-square, coefficient of variation), probabilistic approaches (Bayesian networks, Hidden Markov Models), and supervised/unsupervised machine learning methods for anomaly detection and fraud classification. A comparative analysis of leading platforms is provided, emphasizing their architectures, detection mechanisms, and technology stacks. The results demonstrate that effective fraud prevention requires the integration of statistical analysis, AI/ML methods, and inter-operator cooperation. The paper concludes with future directions, including real-Time graph analytics and Explainable AI.  © 2025 IEEE.},
