	affiliations = {Srm Institute of Science and Technology, School of Computing, Tamilnadu, Tiruchirapalli, India; Chhatrapati Shahu Ji Maharaj University (CSJMU), Department of Computer Application, Uttar Pradesh, Kanpur, India; Cgc University, School of Engineering & Technology, Punjab, Mohali, India; Sona College of Technology, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Tamilnadu, Salem, India; Chandigarh University, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Mohali, India; Graphic Era (Deemed to be University), Department of Electronics and Communication Engineering, Uttarakhand, Dehradun, India},
	abstract = {Loss of transparency and bias in the detection of financial fraud presents two significant problems, namely, poor adherence to the standards of fraud detection, and lost user confidence. In order to address these issues, we have come up with a Multi-Stage XAI-Enhanced Transformer Model that is used to deal with bias-conscious decision making in fraud detection. We use a combination of three aspects Transformers to identify complex pattern of temporal frauds, adversarial debiasing to make fair predictions across the various demographic groups and explainability by visualization of attention and SHAP analysis. We achieve 97.3% accuracy, 98.1% recall, and 99.2% AUC-ROC, better than current algorithms, such as XGBoost (95.1%) and Random Forests (93.8%). Better still, we maintained demographic parity disparity at less than 0.02 which reduced bias by 89.8 per cent relative to conventional systems. The SHAP analysis revealed that the most decisions are made basing on the Transaction Amount, Time of Transaction, and Merchant Category which provides the stakeholders with a clear understanding of the operation of the model. Such a balance between explainability and bias minimization forms a fraud protection system that is both non-discriminatory and credible enough, as required by the regulations and ethical principles of the fintech sector.  © 2026 IEEE.},
