	abstract = {Increased use of digital financial transactions has made it easier to commit fraudulent transactions, and hence, it negates the traditional rule-based detection systems that fail to detect new fraud patterns. The goal of the research is to enhance the detection of financial fraud using an adaptive machine learning method that integrates transaction-based features, relational modeling, as well as interpretable outputs. The pipeline comprises procuring and pre-processing high-dimensional transactional data, engineering features, training supervised models for deep learning, and F1-score, recall, precision, and accuracy performance metrics. The developed framework obtained an overall accuracy of 95.8, and precision, recall, and F1-Score over 92.0, superior to current approaches for financial fraud detection, such as transaction-level, graph neural network, explainable machine learning, unsupervised anomaly detection, and systematic machine learning solutions. In these findings, the framework could detect both regular fraud and evasive fraud patterns without losing the ability to present an interpretable and scalable to the real-world, explanation of the features. Overall, the research establishes that explainable, adaptive machine learning is extremely valuable to augment financial fraud detection, reduce false-positives, and equip professionals with actionable data to assist operational decision-making, which represents an actionable remedy to mitigate the escalating use of financial systems for increasingly sophisticated fraud in the financial sector.  © 2025 IEEE.},
