	affiliations = {Vellore Institute of Technology, Computer Science and Engineering (SCOPE), Chennai, India},
	abstract = {Secure management of digital asset ownership has been recognized as one of the main challenges in decentralized systems, especially when considering fraudulent transaction behaviors. Although blockchain technology ensures immutable and auditable records, it fails to provide the capabilities required to proactively detect suspicious activities. In this study, a new approach for detecting fraud in the domain of blockchain-enabled digital assets is proposed, which leverages Ethereum smart contracts combined with a novel fraud detection module comprising machine learning algorithms, graph mining, and temporal feature extraction to identify fraudster behavior. Behavioral modeling within operations, such as the transfer, validation, and registration of transactions, is included in the proposed framework to cover various types of fraudulent attacks. Cost-aware logging of transactions has also been proposed to efficiently record suspicious transactions in the blockchain without any overhead. Experimental evaluation on the Elliptic Bitcoin dataset showed that the proposed hybrid blockchain fraud detection framework achieved a ROC-AUC score of 0.998 and fraud recall of 95% under class imbalance condition. The use of XAI methods is aimed at ensuring transparency in decision-making processes, whereas the security analysis is based on the use of the STRIDE methodology. The proposed framework can serve as an interpretable and scalable method for addressing the issues of securing digital-asset-tracking systems deployed within blockchain environments. © 2026 IEEE.},
