	abstract = {This paper presents an interpretable machine learning framework for credit card fraud detection that integrates ensemble tree-based algorithms with SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) analysis. We evaluate eight machine learning models-including XGBoost, LightGBM, Gradient Boosting, CatBoost, Random Forest, AdaBoost, Extra Trees, and Decision Tree-on a large-scale European credit card transaction dataset containing over 550,000 records. Our results demonstrate exceptional performance, with XGBoost, Gradient Boosting, and LightGBM achieving perfect AUC scores of 0.99 on test data. To address the”black box” limitation of ensemble models, we employ comprehensive SHAP analysis including feature importance rankings, waterfall plots, force plots, and dependence plots. The SHAP analysis reveals that features V14, V4, and V12 are most influential in fraud detection decisions and demonstrates complex non-linear relationships between feature values and fraud predictions. This framework provides financial institutions with both high-accuracy fraud detection and transparent, feature-level explanations essential for regulatory compliance and operational decision-making. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
