	abstract = {Credit card fraud costs the global economy over $30 billion annually, intensifying the need for effective detection systems. Although most studies emphasize accuracy or visualization, few assess advanced deep learning methods that jointly address accuracy, uncertainty, and interpretability, which are essential capabilities for trusted financial systems. This study investigates the effectiveness of advanced deep learning models: graph attention networks (GAT), graph SAGE, Bayesian neural networks (BNN) and graph convolutional networks (GCN), compared to a statistical baseline, logistic regression, for detecting fraudulent banking transactions. Using a common transaction dataset, we evaluate models on predictive performance, estimation uncertainty, and explainability through visualization.BNNs achieved the highest F1 score (0.890) with robust uncertainty quantification, making them ideal for risk-sensitive settings. GATs attained the highest AUC (0.988) and provided interpretable attention-based visualizations that reveal suspicious transaction networks. Our analysis shows that BNNs are preferable when minimizing false positives is critical, while GATs excel at detecting coordinated fraud. This work offers a systematic, uncertainty-aware, and explainable evaluation of state-of-the-art deep learning models for fraud detection, paving the way for advances in explainable AI within the financial domain. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
