	affiliations = {Moon Shepherd Baker Insurance Agency, Inc, Houston, 77018, TX, United States},
	abstract = {Artificial intelligence is increasingly used in insurance to support underwriting, premium pricing, claims management, fraud detection, and catastrophe risk modeling. Although machine learning methods have shown strong predictive performance in these areas, many deployments remain fragmented, with models and data pipelines implemented as isolated components and limited support for explainability, monitoring, and operational governance. This fragmentation reduces consistency, complicates regulatory review, and slows integration into production decision workflows. This paper presents a Unified Insurance AI Platform (UIAP), a cloud-native architecture that supports end-to-end insurance analytics within a single operational framework. UIAP integrates multi-source data ingestion, centralized feature management, model development and deployment, explainability services, performance monitoring, and business-facing decision dashboards. The platform supports both batch and real-time inference through a shared pipeline with built-in governance controls and auditability features. A case study based on representative insurance analytics workflows is used to evaluate the platform, demonstrating improved operational efficiency, consistent model management, and increased transparency in decision support. The proposed platform provides a practical blueprint for scalable and governance-aware insurance AI systems.Impact Statement - Property and casualty insurance organizations increasingly rely on advanced analytics to support underwriting, pricing, claims management, fraud detection, and catastrophe risk assessment. However, many insurers continue to operate fragmented analytical systems that limit scalability, transparency, and timely decision support. The unified insurance AI platform presented in this paper illustrates how cloud-native architectures and integrated operational practices can consolidate multiple insurance analytics functions within a single environment. By unifying data ingestion, model lifecycle management, explainability, and decision dashboards, the platform supports improved operational efficiency, analytical consistency, and governance across insurance workflows. Evaluation results indicate favorable performance characteristics, reduced operational complexity, and increased transparency in high-impact insurance decisions under representative conditions. Beyond insurance, the architectural principles described in this work provide a transferable reference for deploying scalable and governance-aware AI systems in other regulated financial services domains where auditability and oversight are essential.  © 2026 IEEE.},
