	abstract = {The rapid global adoption of real-time payment systems like the Unified Payments Interface (UPI) has created unprecedented convenience but has also exposed significant vulnerabilities to financial fraud. This necessitates advanced detection systems that are not only accurate but also private, transparent, and exceptionally fast. Existing research often addresses these requirements in isolation. A key novelty of our study is the introduction of prediction latency as a first class metric, directly addressing the real-time constraints of financial systems a factor commonly overlooked. This paper introduces a holistic, three pillar framework designed for the practical deployment of UPI fraud detection. We conduct a rigorous comparative study of diverse machine learning architectures including Logistic Regression, Multi-Layer Perceptrons, and XGBoost to empirically identify an optimal model based on a trade-off analysis of performance and speed. The empirically validated optimal model is then implemented in a comprehensive framework featuring Federated Learning (FL) for privacy-preserving training and Explainable AI (XAI) for transparency. Our results reveal that XGBoost provides a superior balance, achieving high fraud recall with significantly lower latency than neural network alternatives, making it a more viable candidate for real-world deployment. ©2026 IEEE.},
