	abstract = {Credit card fraud poses a critical challenge to the financial sector, requiring accurate and interpretable detection systems. Most existing research in this domain relies on limited, overused datasets with insufficient feature variety, which restricts model generalizability and performance. To address this limitation, we present a novel feature-rich dataset created by aggregating and engineering 38 diverse features drawn from multiple publicly available sources, which is publicly available for research purposes at: https://github.com/KetanAgrawal11/credit-card-fraud-xaidataset. This dataset incorporates transaction behavior, temporal patterns, user profiling, and derived statistical indicators, enabling a deeper understanding of fraudulent activities. We apply and compare several machine learning algorithms - including Logistic Regression, Random Forest, XGBoost, LightGBM, and Gradient Boosting - to evaluate predictive performance. Among these, Gradient Boosting outperformed other models, achieving an Area Under the ROC Curve (AUC) score of 0.9463. In addition, we integrate Explainable AI (XAI) techniques to interpret model decisions, enhancing trust and transparency in the fraud detection process. Our findings demonstrate that a thoughtfully constructed feature-rich dataset, combined with robust ML models and explainability tools, significantly improves the accuracy and interpretability of credit card fraud detection systems. © 2025 IEEE.},
