	journal = {2026 5th MEC International Conference on Advanced Data Analytics and Artificial Intelligence for Sustainable Smart Cities, ICADAAI 2026},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/ICADAAI64945.2026.11679194},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105052631622?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Global College of Engineering and Technology (GCET), Department of Computer Science and Creative Technologies (CSCT), Muscat, Oman},
	abstract = {Credit card fraud detection is still an enormous challenge for financial institutions, the monetary consequences associated with not detecting fraud, and ever-increasing regulatory requirements for transparency, credit card fraud detection continues to be a major challenge for financial institutions. Although machine learning models have demonstrated strong predictive capabilities, their adoption in real-world financial systems is often limited by a lack of interpretability and accountability. The current study proposes an Explainable AI-Driven Credit Card Fraud Detection System based on the Explainable Fraud Risk Algorithm (XFRA). XFRA defines the system's core decision logic by integrating probabilistic fraud scoring, recall-oriented threshold optimisation, and explainable artificial intelligence. An ensemble-based classifier optimised for imbalanced data is employed, with F2-score-based threshold selection prioritising fraud recall while maintaining acceptable precision. Model interpretability is ensured through SHAP and LIME, providing both global and transaction-level explanations. To evaluate operational performance, a real-time transaction monitor and alert system simulation was created. The results of the simulation indicate the ability of this proposed system to provide robust fraud detection, as well as transparent and auditable explanations that allow for additional review and documentation required by analysts and regulators. © 2026 IEEE.},
