	abstract = {Financial fraud has been complicated in the digital era. This has resulted in a dramatic loss of revenue and also security challenges. Conventional detection of fraud systems are typically unable to keep up with emerging fraud patterns due to their preset rule base. This paper implements the Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) machine learning classification algorithm which is effective, scalable and suits the detection of imbalanced data to generate a real-time fraud detection machine. The proposed approach involves tuning of the hyper parameters, feature engineering and painstaking handling of the data used. It addresses the balancing of the data on transactions through such methods as cost-sensitive learning and SMOTE. Tested on actual financial data, the system attains high performance with such metrics as precision, recall, F1- score, and AUC-ROC. In the digital economy, financial frauds are huge concerns. Cybercriminals are adopting ever sophisticated methods to capitalise on the vulnerabilities in systems of online financial systems. The velocity and multidimensionality of the present-day financial transactions indicate that the conventional fraud detection strategies, which tend to be inert and rule-intensive are no longer effective. Such systems lack real-time fraud prevention capability because they have very high false positive rates, cannot easily scale up, and have long reaction times. To guarantee effective antifraud detection and fraud resilience to evolving cyberthreats, adaptive learning and explainability of the model has additionally been integrated into a real-time monitoring system. © 2025 IEEE.},
