	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036990581?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Kalasalingam Academy of Research and Education, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Krishnankoil, Virudhunagar, India},
	abstract = {Modern financial institutions are under greater pressure to not only identify a fraudulent action but also to generate transparent credit evaluation, and one in which the regulation is not compromised. Modern pipelines generally approach these as discrete tasks which results in disjointed attribute mapping and black box behaviours not meeting the regulatory requirement of traceability. In this paper, the authors propose a single framework (called Dual-Objective Interpretable Ensemble) that simultaneously fraud stratifies and scores credit using a single interpretable framework. DOIE combines a diversity-optimal ensemble backbone with a hierarchical say layer, resorting to monotonicity based constrained learners, trajectory union between local and global attribution, as well as sample boundary singleness characterization. The outputs of the system are synchronized: a categorical risk of fraud-risk and a continuous credit rating and a report to the regulator consolidating rule-level evidence. The methodology has the benefit of providing every judgment with an equivalent and evidenced analytical rationale. Experimental analysis of multimodal financial data shows that DOIE is able to reach an F1-score of 0.962 with an association rate of 0.011 on fraud detection, as well as an AUC of 0.947 on credit default prediction. Besides, the framework retains over 0.91 accountability scores in all elements. These findings confirm that DOIE is a powerful framework that can reconcile high-performance predictive analytics with the demands of financial compliance that are highly interpretable.  © 2026 IEEE.},
