	affiliations = {Ajay Kumar Garg Engineering College, Ghaziabad, India},
	abstract = {As the online shopping in the world expands, so is the digital fraud. By the end of 2025, merchants will probably have lost about $ 48 billion, including those that they lost in 2023. It is reported by the industry that the market of e-commerce fraud detection will increase to $25.92 billion in 2032 and it will be $6.97 billion in 2025. This paper suggests an effective multi-level AI system to avoid E-Commerce fraud. Our focus lies on three types of fraud and those are transactional fraud, collusive network fraud and account takeover or bot attacks. The framework is responsible for learning patterns from transaction data, account relationships and device activity due to models like XGBoost, shallow neural networks and graph analysis. This helps in identifying high-risk transactions and uncovering hidden fraud networks. The system also uses unsupervised models like Isolation Forest and One-Class SVM and behavioral biometrics to spot interaction patterns or unusual login. This helps in detecting accounts that may have been taken over or some other bot activities. Our system uses a stacking ensemble, in which a final model combines the outputs of various models into one to get the fraud risk score. The proposed stacking architecture addresses the extreme class imbalance (0.093% fraud) by combining supervised, unsupervised and structural risk signals. After our system evaluation on imbalanced fraud dataset, we achieved an AUPRC of 0.3517 and a specificity of 99.45%. Analysis of meta-learner weights (βXGB ≈ 5.44) confirms the dominance of transactional evidence while successfully filtering noisy behavioral features which minimize false positives showing more clarity on how decisions are made, which makes the system reliable for future explainable fraud prevention in e-commerce. © 2026 IEEE.},
