	affiliations = {Gopalan College of Engineering and Management, Dept. of Computer Science and Engineering, Bengaluru, India},
	abstract = {Online recruitment platforms have made job search convenient but have also increased exposure to fraudulent job postings that steal personal data or money. This paper presents XAI-Recruit, an end-to-end explainable AI system for detecting recruitment fraud using deep learning, designed to both classify job postings as fake or real and to provide human-interpretable explanations for its decisions. Our approach combines a text classification model based on a bidirectional LSTM [10] trained on the Fake Job Postings dataset (with class imbalance addressed via oversampling), with a lightweight explainability layer using LIME [1] to highlight the words that most influenced each prediction. In addition, XAI-Recruit incorporates a rule-based job-type classifier that labels postings as Technical, Non-Technical, or Mixed using domain keyword matching, enabling additional contextual filtering. The system is exposed through a RESTful Flask API and an interactive Next.js frontend to support real-time inspection of job descriptions. Experiments on publicly available recruitment datasets demonstrate the effectiveness of the combined detection + explanation pipeline: the model achieves robust classification performance while the LIME explanations provide concise, token-level rationales that align with human intuition. We discuss implementation details, limitations of local surrogate explainers, and directions for improving both detection and interpretability (e.g., transformer models and SHAP integration). © 2026 IEEE.},
