	affiliations = {Kakatiya Institute of Technology and Science, India},
	abstract = {Credit cards are widely used for online purchases due to their efficiency and ease. However, there is now a greater chance of credit card theft and abuse due to this increasing usage. In this paper, develop a fraud detection system using LLMs in conjunction with traditional machine learning algorithms. The system design process involves various steps, including data pre-processing, feature engineering, anomaly detection, time-based split, and SMOTE sampling. Transactions are transformed into textual inputs to be used by BERT for modeling and then incorporated with tabular features using hybrid and ensemble methods, which include Hybrid Tab+BERT, BERT-led Meta Stack, and BERT-emb + XGBoost. Experimental analysis reveals that Hybrid Tab+BERT produces the optimal results with PR-AUC = 0.6777, ROC-AUC = 0.9608, and F1-score = 0.6337, whereas BERT-led Meta Stack has the highest recall of 0.9551 at the lowest cost ($1610). However, BERT-emb + XGBoost yields inferior outcomes with a PR-AUC of 0.4251. In addition, the explanations provided by LLMs improve the interpretability of the system. This research introduces an intelligent and scalable AI framework for enhancing predictive capabilities. The findings demonstrate improved accuracy, precision, stability, and address problems like low recall and overfitting. It also exhibits good generalization and deployment capabilities.  © 2026 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Anomaly Detection; BERT; Credit Card Fraud Detection; Explainable AI; Large Language Models (LLMs); SMOTE},
