	affiliations = {Ilmenau University of Technology, Ilmenau, Germany; AiVader GmbH, Ilmenau, Germany; Cuculus GmbH, Ilmenau, Germany},
	abstract = {Smart meters in power grids enable data-driven operations, yet electricity theft and tampering cause major non-technical losses. Many machine learning (ML) models achieve high accuracy but act as black boxes, limiting operator trust. We present an explainable AI (xAI) framework that augments supervised (Random Forest, CatBoost) and unsupervised (VAE, LSTM-AE) baselines with attribution-driven feature selection. Using daily readings from more than 40,000 customers over three years, we show that xAI reduces dimensionality and inference latency without degrading detection quality. The method highlights abnormal consumption, sudden drops, and time-of-use anomalies, surfacing customers and days for field inspections. CatBoost and LSTM-AE emerge as most effective supervised and unsupervised baselines, respectively, with about 400 selected features balancing accuracy and speed. Explainability is introduced without performance loss, supporting faster, auditable, and trusted utility operations.  © 2025 IEEE.},
