	abstract = {Food fraud represents a worldwide escalating threat because it affects public health management alongside consumer safety as well as economic losses. Artificial Intelligence (AI) functions as a powerful investigative tool because traditional food authentication methods fail to discover modern complex food scams. Deep Learning (DL) models prove effective for boosting accuracy together with the efficiency of food fraud detection systems. The research investigates how Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) works with DL models used to analyze food authenticity. DL uses Convolutional neural networks and Recurrent neural networks and generative models as components to perform complete food composition analysis while identifying adulterants and detecting irregularities in both spectral and imaging data series. XAI plays a critical part in making these models easy to understand because it uses Shapley Additive Explanations and Local Interpretable Model-Agnostic Explanations tools to reveal both decision mechanisms and the importance of features. The paper explores the cooperative effect of AI with cutting-edge spectroscopy systems like Near-Infrared, Hyperspectral Imaging, and Raman Spectroscopy when these systems deliver precise compositions of food materials. The field still encounters multiple difficulties including complex computability and live computation and approval regulations together with moral dilemmas. © 2025 IEEE.},
