	affiliations = {VA, United States; NC, United States; TX, United States},
	abstract = {The role of Machine Learning (ML) models has grown substantially in the detection of fraud and abuse in healthcare claims data. However, the lack of a unified testing and validation framework complicates the problems of reliability, transparency, fairness, and regulatory compliance. This paper develops a Unified Testing Framework (UTF) designed specifically for validating ML-based fraud detection systems in healthcare claims processing. The framework includes modules for model performance evaluation, integrity of data validation, adversarial robustness, explainability, and compliance verification. Using a hybrid approach consisting of statistical testing, adversarial simulation, and explainable AI (XAI) metrics, we develop a rigorous methodology by which to assess whether a model is sufficiently mature to be deployed in real-world payer environments. Experimental results from simulated healthcare claims datasets show that the UTF improves accuracy by 8-12% while reducing bias and false positives compared to conventional ad hoc validation methods. The proposed framework is well aligned with industry standards such as HIPAA, GDPR, and the CMS program integrity guidelines for ethical deployment of AI. © 2025 IEEE.},
