	abstract = {Due to the swift increase of online banking, electronic financial services, and online payment processing, financial fraud detection has become a burning research topic in contemporary banking. The growing sophistication of financial interactions has rendered traditional regulation-based systems of detecting fraud inadequate in detecting advanced patterns of fraud. Fraud detection methods especially artificial intelligence has also gone a long way to enhance this; although most of the models are not transparent, they cannot be practically applied in financial institutions. The proposed study will be a fraud detection system using Explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) and integrates machine learning classifications and explanations. The data preprocess methodology includes transaction data preprocessing, feature normalization, probabilistic prediction of fraud with a supervised learning model, calculation of anomaly scores, and feature contribution analysis to create explanations suitable to understand the reasons why a specific prediction was made. The experimental analysis has proven that the proposed model has better detection performance than the current methods. The model has an F1-score of 0.90, MCC of 0.82 and AUPRC of 0.89 and beats the hybrid machine learning models with 0.86,0.76 and 0.84 respectively. According to the outcomes, explainability together with machine learning has been found to enhance reliability of fraud detection without jeopardizing transparency of banking security systems.  © 2026 IEEE.},
