	abstract = {As mobile payments evolve toward Embedded Subscriber Identity Module (eSIM)-enabled ecosystems, financial institutions face a pressing question: can Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) techniques proven for credit card fraud detection adapt to emerging threats in converging payment-telecommunications infrastructures? The eSIM technology introduces distinct attack vectors, including SIM swap fraud, unauthorized provisioning, and man-in-the-middle attacks during activation, that existing systems were not designed to address, while regulatory demands for transparent AI decisions remain constant. This study presents the Risk Understanding in Monetary Intelligence (RUMI) framework, which integrates supervised models, SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP)-based interpretability, and TinyLlama-1.1B for natural language explanations. Dual-domain evaluation validates the approach on real-world credit card data, yielding a receiver operating characteristic area under the curve (ROC-AUC) of 0.997, and provides preliminary evidence of adaptability to synthetic eSIM infrastructure (ROC-AUC: 1.000). SHAP-based analysis reveals behavioral patterns dominating payment fraud and cryptographic attributes driving eSIM security, offering complementary intelligence for converged protection strategies. The framework addresses payment fraud and explainability challenges central to e-commerce and mobile commerce operations. © IFIP International Federation for Information Processing 2027.},
