	abstract = {In light of the data complexity and regulatory demands, artificial intelligence (AI) is becoming an important tool in financial risk management. This paper is informed by a systematic literature review of recent peer-reviewed articles aimed at understanding the use of AI in credit, market, fraud, and operational risks. Of the studies, over 40% focused on credit risk, where AI techniques such as decision trees and neural networks utilized alternative data to improve default prediction. Around 22% focused on market risk, using deep learning and sentiment analysis to develop forecasts for volatility and resilience in portfolios. Fraud represented 18% of the sample, where AI performed well with anomaly detection and behavioral profiling. The literature has improved in producing studies with clear findings and isolated definitions of AI and other emerging technologies. However, few studies, less than 20% of the papers reviewed, address ethical and regulatory issue, such as algorithmic bias or explainability. The findings demonstrate the affordances and constraints present when using AI systems while emphasising the need for models to be explainable, robust validation, and a transparent governance framework. All financial institutions should be proactive, evidence-led, and socially responsible in the integration of AI while encouraging innovation balanced with accountability to build long-term financial resilience. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.},
