	year = {2026},
	journal = {2026 International Conference on Sustainable Engineering and Technology Innovations, ICSETI 2026},
	pages = {531 - 536},
	doi = {10.1109/ICSETI67678.2026.11637015},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047893335?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Dayananda Sagar Academy of Technology and Management, Computer Science and Engineering, Bengaluru, India},
	abstract = {Unified Payments Interface (UPI) is used for most of the payments in India. But as the usage of UPI is increasing, so are the fraudulent activities related to it. There are some solutions which address this problem. These solutions make use of centralized anomaly detection to detect transactional fraud. But the issue with these methods is that they don't provide much data privacy. Using the idea of Federated Learning and Explainable AI for fraud detection, this paper overcomes the drawbacks of the centralized models. Our model allows banks to work with their partners and train their own local models without sharing any raw data. The proposed model uses a synthetic UPI transactions dataset that replicates the real-world transactional data. Through evaluation of this dataset, results show a successful detection rate of 94.8%, with precision equating to 92.3% and recall to 89.7%, while compounding a 41.2% reduction to the false positive rate compared to centralized anomalies. The solution processes all transactions in under 120ms for potential real-time payment decisions derived from privacy-preserving, distributed, model training.  © 2026 IEEE.},
