	title = {Explainability in Multi-Relational Opinion Spam Detection: The Trade-Offs of XAI-Guided Graph Pruning},
	year = {2026},
	journal = {Procedia Computer Science},
	volume = {289},
	pages = {1046 - 1055},
	doi = {10.1016/j.procs.2026.08.324},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105052806586?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Computer Science Department, Semarang Campus, School of Computer Science, Bina Nusantara University, Jakarta, 11480, Indonesia},
	abstract = {Online reviews have a major role in shaping consumer behavior. The growing presence of fake reviews represents a serious threat to e-commerce platforms. Fraudsters have become more sophisticated, adopting strategies that mimic real user behavior. Graph Neural Networks still have a challenge to provide a solution to this problem. This study examines how Explainable AI (XAI) can be applied to active graph pruning within a MRGA inspired multi-relational architecture by using the heavily imbalanced Yelp fraud dataset. Two XAI approaches were compared: GNNExplainer, a local method, and PGExplainer, a global one. The results reveal a difference in how well each method preserves graph structure. The baseline model achieved a Spam F1-score of 0.65. GNNExplainer managed to retain important relational patterns which delivered an F1-Score of 0.64 and 0.65 while keeping 58.3% and 59% of direct edges intact on the two test runs. On the other hand, PGExplainer, showed a performance drop that its F1-score was only 0.50 and 0.55, due to extreme pruning of the edges with only 38.20% and 31.60% of direct edges surviving. We theoretically attribute this failure to mathematical regularization collapse within the global explainer, compounded by the 'Faithfulness Trap' inherent in optimizing XAI purely against baseline predictions rather than ground-truth labels. Even though inductive XAI remains computationally essential for large-scale streaming networks, our findings highlight the urgent need for future methods to integrate noise-robust and sound reasoning to prevent destructive graph pruning. © 2026 The Author(s).},
	author_keywords = {Explainable AI (XAI); Graph Neural Networks; Multi-Relational Graphs; Opinion Spam Detection; Relational Camouflage; Trust and Safety},
