	affiliations = {Abu Dhabi Polytechnic, Information Security Engineering Technology (ISET), Abu Dhabi, United Arab Emirates},
	abstract = {Credit card fraud poses escalating risks in digital payment systems, with global losses exceeding $32 billion annually. Traditional rule-based detection systems suffer from 15-20% false positive rates and poor adaptability, while academic machine learning models often neglect real-Time deployment, regulatory explainability, and operational workflow integration. This paper presents EFDS (Ensemble Fraud Detection System), a production-ready platform combining Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, and Logistic Regression through soft voting, achieving 94.2% accuracy, 89% precision, and 92% recall with sub-150ms inference latency. The system integrates 14 ML modules (3,453 lines) analyzing eight engineered features including geographic origin, merchant risk, device novelty, and transaction velocity across 21 frontend pages and 10 backend API modules. High-risk transactions (score ≥ 70) trigger automated investigation workflows with CBUAE-compliant explainability. Validation across 116 tests achieving 91% code coverage confirms OWASP Top 10 security compliance and deployment readiness for UAE financial institutions. Live deployment at https://fraudguard.tech demonstrates that ensemble learning integrated with regulatory transparency and analyst workflows enables operationally viable fraud prevention. © 2026 IEEE.},
