	affiliations = {Inpt, EM2TI, Rabat, Morocco},
	abstract = {The increasing adoption of opaque AI models in financial fraud detection creates a fundamental tension between predictive performance and regulatory accountability. The EU AI Act (2026) has codified this tension into law by mandating a "Right to Explanation"for high-risk automated decisions, making explainability a legal requirement rather than a design preference. This survey provides a structured analysis of Explainable AI (XAI) methods applied to financial fraud detection. We propose a taxonomy organizing the literature across three data modalities, tabular, graph, and temporal, and introduce the "Explainability-Imbalance Paradox,"which shows that standard resampling techniques can corrupt the fidelity of post-hoc explanations. We further examine emergent frontiers including Self-Explaining Neural Networks (SENN), Generative XAI driven by Large Language Models, and Privacy-Preserving XAI via federated learning. Synthesizing over 130 recent works, we identify open challenges in real-time scalability and adversarial robustness, and outline a strategic roadmap for deploying compliant, trustworthy financial intelligence systems. © 2026 IEEE.},
