	affiliations = {University of Tehran, Electrical and Computer Engineering, Tehran, Iran; University of Tehran, School of Mechanical Engineering, Tehran, Iran; Civil Engineering Sejong University, Seoul, South Korea; Sejong University, Computer Science and Engineering, Seoul, South Korea},
	abstract = {Credit card fraud (CCF) has been recognized as a growing issue in the financial industry as a result of the number of fraud-related activities that have been on the upswing. The existing machine learning (ML) solutions are mainly based on traditional classifiers or simple ensemble solutions and often cannot detect complex, non-linear patterns of fraud. This paper suggests an improvement to CCF detection with the addition of two attention layers to a traditional stacking classifier. Two superior aggregation operators dependent ordered weighted averaging (DOWA) and induced ordered weighted averaging (IOWA), are used in the first attention layer to mix the soft outputs of first-level classifiers; DOWA helps to reduce the impact of the outliers by placing heterogeneous weight of the samples whereas IOWA uses gradual weight allocation through the application of a gradient-based optimization procedure similar to the neural network process. The second attention layer uses mechanism of synergistic combination that is developed to detect the most trustworthy aggregated value that will be transmitted to a meta-learner to make final prediction. The conducted experimental findings on a large-scale dataset of credit-card transactions indicate that the suggested model outperforms traditional ensemble classifiers, achieving a detection rate of 94.45% and area under the curve (AUC) of 0.97, which makes it essential to note that the suggested model is the most effective when working in the context of a multifaceted concept of fraud detection.  © 2025 IEEE.},
