	abstract = {Online shopping is increasing, but many online reviews are fake, which misleads customers and damages businesses' credibility. In this work, MetaWeave is described as the framework that has been developed for the more reliable detection of fake reviews. Instead of looking at text alone, as many older systems do, MetaWeave examines four types of clues: how the review is written, the time at which it was posted, how the reviewer and product are connected in the network, and also the behavior patterns of the reviewer. When these pieces are combined, the system gets a clearer picture whether a review is real or not. MetaWeave was tested on the OSF Fake Reviews dataset, containing 40,432 reviews across ten product groups. On the test split, MetaWeave reaches 95.0 percent ROC-AUC, an F1-score of 88.17 percent, and an accuracy of 88.20 percent. To avoid data leakage and to reflect real deployment conditions, a user-level data partitioning strategy is also introduced that ensures reviews from the same user do not appear in both training and testing sets. An interpretability study with SHAP and LIME is also provided to identify which linguistic, structural, and behavioral features influence the model's predictions.  © 2026 IEEE.},
