	affiliations = {California State University-Dominguez Hills, United States; University of South Dakota, United States},
	abstract = {Online banking, credit card purchases, and mobile wallet are all examples of the growing popularity of banking activities. Financial organizations, retailers, and consumers all face the formidable problem of detecting fraudulent financial transactions. The Online Payments Fraud Detection dataset, trained using ML, is used in this paper to offer an approach for identifying fraudulent online payments. The dataset is preprocessed by sequentially applying process such as work with missing data, trimming outliers (by percentile) and label encoding, feature scaling (for StandardScaler) and class balancing (using SMOTE) to deal with the gross class imbalance issue. The resulting data are partitioned into training and testing data and various machine learning models, that is, XGBoost, Extra Trees Classifier (ETC), and Hybrid Anomaly–CatBoost Fusion model, are trained to recognize frauds. The measures to determine the performance of the models include accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, ROC-AUC, MCC, and specificity. Based on the experimental findings, the proposed hybrid model has better performance with an accuracy of 0.9990, precision of 0.9997, recall of 0.9984, and ROC-AUC of 0.9999, compared to other models. Moreover, explainable AI is based on SHAP to explain the model predictions and the most significant transaction features. The findings reveal that the suggested hybrid model is an effective and dependable remedy to identify suspicious online activities and enhance financial cyber defense mechanisms. ©2026 IEEE.},
