	abstract = {Credit card fraud is an economic problem that requires efficient detection. We addressed this challenge using a publicly available, highly imbalanced dataset with features anonymized via Principal Component Analysis (PCA). We evaluate how feature engineering helps involving cyclical time encoding, log transform on transaction amounts, binning, and polynomial interactions between important PCA components that affects model performance. Multiple machine learning models (Logistic Regression, Random Forest, XGBoost) were tested with varying imbalanced handling schemes, such as baseline, class weighting, and SMOTE. We highlight that engineered features, particularly in combination with SMOTE, considerably enhance the performance of the untuned ensemble models. Random Forest and XGBoost with SMOTE had the highest scores. After tuning, the optimal combination, XGBoost with scale-pos-weight and engineered features, obtained an AUPRC of 0.8826 and a recall of 0.8469. SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) analysis indicated that interaction terms between PCA components were top predictors, providing transparency independent of anonymization. These results highlight the benefits of feature engineering, model calibration, and explainability methods in achieving improved fraud detection in imbalanced real-world data. © 2025 IEEE.},
