	affiliations = {School of Computer Applications, Manav Rachna International Institute of Research & Studies, Faridabad, India},
	abstract = {Fraud is another risk that has continued to pose a very serious threat to the insurance industry, as it damages the insurer's financial base and also erodes consumer trust. Thus, this research aims at establishing the effectiveness of artificial intelligence in increasing the audit trail for more effective fraud detection while keeping clients at the core. It effectively stills the perennial conflict between fraud detection, a massive area of concern within this ever-learning insurance environment, and clients' impressions. This study uses quantitative data from three insurance companies' claims data collected through machine learning methods, as well as qualitative data from 50 insurance company employees and 200 customers. Data shows that our proposed AI-based model is better than the existing rule-based model in terms of detecting more frauds, reducing false positives by 28 percent and increasing detection rates by 37 percent. Finally, the explainable AI parts of the system enable reasonable actions based on the algorithm and allay customers' concerns about bias. From the overall findings made previously, it can be concluded that customers are willing to accept the use of artificial intelligence in detecting fraud when the procedure is well explained to them or when they can appeal the decision made by the artificial intelligence. Therefore, this research provides a guide to customer-orientated AI use in fraud detection, and such information will be useful for insurance firms willing to adopt cutting-edge technologies in a socially responsible manner. © 2025 IEEE.},
