	year = {2026},
	journal = {4th International Conference on Sustainable Computing and Smart Systems, ICSCSS 2026 - Proceedings},
	pages = {1159 - 1166},
	doi = {10.1109/ICSCSS69635.2026.11645852},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048330045?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Pes University, Dept. of Computer Applications, Banglore, India},
	abstract = {In footwear industry Counterfeit of footwear continues to be one of the major challenges faced by global brands not only because it exposes consumers to low-quality products but also due to substantial monetary and reputational loss of the companies by lack of customer's trust for the brand. Current solutions provide inadequate transparency and lack any cryptographic association between a physical asset and the digital data related to footwear product. A customized blockchain supply chain management system tailored for the footwear industry, FootChain uses the Proof of Authority (PoA) approach with three trusted validatory nodes where the authority of each validator is based on listing their public keys (via ECDSA algorithm). All events occurring along the supply chain such as submission by authorized parties including manufacturer, warehouse manager, logistics provider, and seller are stored in the form of transactions forming blocks linked with each other via SHA-256 hash cryptographic function creating an immutable chain. MongoDB is used as the operational database for instant data retrieval, complex queries, and storing of binary QR codes. Every individual has its physical product cryptographically tied to its unique ECDSA signature during manufacturing and receives two QR codes: the private QR code, which is AES-128 encrypted and available exclusively to the authorized supply chain roles to submit events updating simultaneously and the public QR code, printed in the packaging of the products for end consumers to verify their product's journey. QR code creation is entirely automated with structured information provided by the manufacturer manifest during batch QR generation by creating and signing large numbers of unit records automatically generating QR codes using qrcode from python library. The anomaly detection model utilizes an Entropy Deep Belief Network tuned via Lévy-flight Grasshopper Optimization Algorithm (EDBN-LGOA) to detect anomalies in ten different supply chain behavior attributes that include QR code cloning, theft of transaction hash-codes and complete fraud entry with a detection rate of 96.4%, compared to 88.7% achieved by the SVM baseline method. To facilitate informed decisions for manufacturers, an Explainable AI (XAI) component employs the use of SHAP values to provide the reasoning for each anomaly score in the end.  © 2026 IEEE.},
