	abstract = {The rapid adoption of UPI in India has been accompanied by a surge in fraudulent activities with nearly one in five Indian households being affected by it, often leading to irreversible financial losses. Recent studies have focussed on machine learning, deep learning, reinforcement learning and anomaly detection techniques, frequently using oversampling techniques like SMOTE to tackle the imbalanced dataset problem. They rely on offline batch training, lack the ability to adapt to emerging fraud patterns, have high false positive rates, lack interpretability, and were not assessed in real time scenarios. This study proposes a continuous learning framework using Passive Aggressive classifier trained on a sliding window, integrated with SMOTE-ENN for class balancing and SHAP for transparency. The framework was analyzed on a user transaction simulation, giving a high macro F1 score and low false positive rate, making it suitable for real-time fraud detection needs. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2027.},
