	title = {A Hybrid Stacking Framework for Corporate Fraud Detection using Dual-Layer Explainable AI},
	year = {2026},
	journal = {Proceedings of 7th International Conference on Intelligent Communication Technologies and Virtual Mobile Networks, ICICV 2026},
	pages = {1033 - 1039},
	doi = {10.1109/ICICV68925.2026.11554767},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105042938016?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Hindustan Institute of Technology and Science, Department of Computer Applications, Chennai, India},
	abstract = {The issue of corporate financial statement fraud has emerged as of great concern to regulatory agencies and the investors given its long-term concealed management of accounting pointers. Conventional auditing, as well as solitary machine learning categorizers, usually cannot detect fraudulent companies successfully due to imbalanced datasets of corporate fraud with a complicated non-linear association amongst financial ratios. In the given work, a Hybrid Stacking Ensemble framework to detect corporate fraud is offered on the basis of a real-world financial statement dataset's consisting of 37,325 samples of that data range between 2021 and 2025, with only 2.6% of the samples being fraudulent. The Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique (SMOTE) is used on the training dataset's in order to solve the issue of class imbalance. The suggested model combines the use of the Random Forest and Gradient Boosting as base learners, and the use of the Logistic Regression as a meta-learner to enhance strength and minimize bias. Moreover, it also includes a dual-layer Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) mechanism featuring SHAP to analyze the importance of global features and LIME to analyze the importance of local instances, allowing to be transparent in their fraud decision. The experimental appraisal shows the proposed structure to be 99.95 accurate, 98.98 precise, 98.98 recall, and 98.98 F1-score, which is excellent in identifying high-risk firms with the fewest amount of false alarms. The suggested solution offers a practical and explainable way of screening and supporting audit decisions in real-time corporate fraud screening. © 2026 IEEE.},
