	abstract = {The design and implementation in this paper consist of an Agentic Enterprise Intelligence architecture that allows secure and automated insurance risk assessment and fraud detection with Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) and Large Language Models (LLMs). The proposed system is realised as a modular agent-based system where specialised autonomous agents carry out an underwriting analysis, claims triage, fraud detection, compliance validation and decision explanation. Structured records and unstructured claim documents constitute enterprise insurance data that are ingested and semantically indexed with the help of vector embeddings in the name of controlled retrieval. Grounded, explainable and regulation compliant decision-making entails dynamically injecting retrieved evidence within the prompts of LLM. Implementation of the zero-trust access control, encrypted retrieval pipelines, prompt-level governance, and end-to-end audit logging is done to address the enterprise security and regulatory needs. Containerized microservices are deployed as end-to-end workflows and scaled and coordinated. The experimental outcomes indicate that the adopted system has a higher level of accuracy in fraud detection, less investigation latency, and higher productivity of the analysts in comparison with the machine learning-based traditional insurance systems. The deployment confirms the practicability of RAG-enabled LLM automation of agentic insurance risk and fraud environments.  © 2026 IEEE.},
