	journal = {Proceedings of the 2026 13th International Conference on Computing for Sustainable Global Development, INDIACom 2026},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.23919/INDIACom70271.2026.11525737},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041829956?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Chalapathi Institute of Technology, Guntur, India},
	abstract = {The growing use of online and mobile banking systems has led to huge volumes of transaction data and subsequent volume of cases of financial frauds. The traditional rule-based, threshold-based systems of detection are usually not effective in identifying changing, subtle and nonlinear frauds. The hybrid ensemble machine learning framework proposed in this paper to overcome these constraints will be one that uses Random Forest (RF), Logistic Regression (LR), and K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) classifiers in conjunction with the Synthetic Minority Oversampling Technique (SMOTE) to balance out the data. The proposed architecture combines the understandability of LR, the strength of RF, and the locality sensitivity of KNN, which allows achieving a trade-off between transparency and detection accuracy. Its workflow is preprocessing, feature encoding, SMOTE-based oversampling, independent model training, and ensemble decision fusion via majority voting. Extensive experiments performed on a synthetically generated banking transaction dataset show that the hybrid ensemble gives an accuracy of 98.4 percent, precision of 0.98, and recall of 0.99 that are better than the single base classifiers and the available benchmark models. Moreover, the suggested structure has steady computational efficiency as well as resistance to class imbalance with a ROC-AUC of 0.99 thereby validating its discriminative power. The interpretability and modular design of the model enable it to be deployed in large-scale real-time banking applications. Continuous adaptive fraud intelligence with explainable AI, temporal sequence modeling, and federated learning are some of the potential extensions in the future. © 2026 Bharati Vidyapeeth's Institute of Computer Applications and Management (BVICAM).},
