	abstract = {The proliferation of generative AI has significantly enhanced deceptive review tactics, posing a critical threat to the integrity of e-commerce platforms. Our study addresses this challenge by integrating information manipulation theory (IMT) with explainable AI techniques to develop a transparent and effective framework for detecting AI-generated deceptive reviews. We systematically generated manipulated reviews using multiple AI-based approaches—real-based generation, fake-based generation, and user-guided—to replicate real-world review fraud scenarios. The primary evaluation focused on detecting mixed manipulated reviews (MMR) versus real reviews, reflecting the complex nature of contemporary online deception in which multiple manipulation strategies coexist. Our findings indicate that features associated with the relation dimension of IMT were particularly effective in distinguishing manipulated content from authentic reviews. The integration of newly adopted features, categorized under all IMT dimensions, contributed to improved detection performance across all types of manipulated reviews, highlighting the strength of a theory-driven and explainable approach. Features related to the relation dimension, such as “Customer Service and Friendliness” and “Restaurant Atmosphere,” were highlighted through interpretability analysis as prominent indicators of deception, pointing to exaggerated positivity as a common strategy in manipulated reviews. These results highlight the increasing sophistication of AI-generated reviews and the pressing need for robust detection frameworks. This study offers a theoretically grounded and empirically validated approach to AI-driven deception detection and provides valuable insights for businesses, regulatory bodies, and online platforms. Our findings contribute to the development of advanced tools for preserving the credibility of online review systems amid the growing prevalence of AI-generated misinformation. © 2025},
