	abstract = {Against the backdrop of the rapid digital transformation of the financial sector and the widespread adoption of electronic payment systems, the problem of money laundering and financial fraud has significantly increased in relevance. Modern criminal schemes are highly dynamic and capable of bypassing traditional rule-based systems, which rely on static thresholds and demonstrate low recall (<0.2%) for fraudulent transactions. This study examines the application of machine learning methods to transaction monitoring using a synthetic dataset of 6.36 million mobile payment transactions with developed features reflecting amounts, account balances, customer behavior, and transaction speed. Baseline models, including logistic regression and random forest, achieve a fraud detection recall of 0.94, with F1 scores for fraudulent transactions ranging from 0.05 (LR) to 0.97 (RF). The XGBoost classifier, trained on a 30 % subsample, achieves an AUC of 0.999, with 99 % of fraud cases concentrated in the high-risk segment, and a false positive rate of only 0.05%, reducing unnecessary alerts by more than 99 %. SHAP-based interpretability revealed that the key factors influencing the model's predictions are the transaction-to-balance ratio and balance dynamics. The results demonstrate that the model effectively detects critical economic risks. Machine learning significantly improves our anti-money laundering capabilities. We can now confidently identify suspicious transactions, minimizing false alerts. This means that risk and compliance professionals will have legally sound and accurate tools to help them make informed decisions.  © 2026 IEEE.},
