	abstract = {Fraud costs banks billions every year - over $10 billion in 2023 according to FTC data. We built a hybrid system combining CNNs, LSTMs, and Random Forest to catch fraudulent credit card transactions. The dataset we used has 284,807 transactions but only 0.17% are fraud, which makes this problem really hard. Our approach works by having CNN-LSTM extract patterns from individual transactions and also from sequences over time, then Random Forest uses these features for final classification. On the test set we got F1-score of 0.80, precision 0.89, and recall 0.72. Cross-validation across five folds gave F1 of 0.388 ± 0.070 and AUC-ROC of 0.96 ± 0.01, showing the model is stable. We added SHAP explanations because banks need to understand why the model flags certain transactions for regulatory reasons. Our false positive rate came out to only 0.019%, so legitimate transactions rarely get blocked - this matters a lot since annoying customers with false declines is a real problem in production systems. © 2026 IEEE.},
