	affiliations = {Computer Engineer, ERC Group Engineering R&D Services, Ankara, Turkey},
	abstract = {Procurement fraud in private enterprise ERP systems is a significant yet chronically underexplored risk. Unlike public procurement—which benefits from regulatory disclosure and external auditing—internal enterprise procurement operates within closed data environments where fraud signals are embedded in transactional workflows, invisible to outsiders. Despite this reality, virtually all automated fraud detection research in the field targets the public sector, leaving private enterprise organizations without purpose-built tools. This paper makes two original contributions to address that gap. First, it introduces transaction chain completeness as an explicit, engineered feature group for ERP fraud detection: a representation of how faithfully a procurement transaction follows the expected procedural sequence of contract, purchase order, goods receipt, invoice, and payment. Second, it proposes a novel price history feature set—derived entirely from the ERP's own purchase records, without any external data dependency—that enables detection of economically motivated fraud that purely procedural controls cannot identify. These features power a two-stage detection pipeline. In Stage 1, an XGBoost classifier scores every transaction using 32 features organized into five groups. In Stage 2, transactions exceeding a configurable risk threshold are escalated to a large language model service that produces auditor-style natural language explanations, identifying the suspected fraud pattern, key risk indicators, and recommended action. The system was trained and evaluated on a synthetic dataset of 10,000 ERP transactions spanning ten labeled fraud patterns. It achieved a cross-validated AUC-ROC of 0.9989 and is implemented as a Python microservice pipeline designed for direct integration into existing healthcare ERP environments. The results validate both the transaction chain completeness concept and the price history feature approach as effective, practically deployable signals for internal procurement fraud detection. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2027.},
