	affiliations = {NJ, United States; Envestnet, Dallas, United States},
	abstract = {In the past few years, Artificial Intelligence (AI)-driven financial systems have transformed and enhanced intelligent banking services, digital payments, and transaction analysis. However, traditional fraud detection systems use machine learning to classify static data and are unable to adapt to changing fraud patterns or balance out the disparity between the amount of available legitimate and fraudulent transactions or to deter evolving cyber-financial attacks. To overcome these challenges, an Automated Adaptive Data and Rule Engineering Framework (AADRE-FD) is proposed for learning how to detect and analyze financial fraud. This data is used to stimulate fraud activity through the utilization of an adversarial balancing scheme based on Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) in which synthesize realistic duplicate fraud transactions to deliver additional training for identifying fraud. After that, a Deep Sparse Autoencoders (DSAEs) for extracting useful features that capture hidden, nonlinear characteristics associated with fraudulent transactions from the high-dimensional space of transactional data. Finally, the AADRE-FD framework will integrate Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) capabilities into the fraud prediction process to promote the transparency and interpretability of decision-making related to predicting fraud. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed AADRE-FD framework achieves 97.96% Accuracy compared with the existing Integrated Multistage Ensemble Machine Learning (IMEML) framework.  © 2026 IEEE.},
