	abstract = {Severe class imbalance significantly hinders the efficacy of credit card fraud detection systems, where fraudulent activities constitute a minute fraction of transaction data. This paper proposes a comprehensive machine learning framework to address this skewness by integrating data-level resampling (SMOTE) with algorithmic-level cost-sensitive learning. We evaluate a suite of supervised and unsupervised models - including Logistic Regression, Random Forest, XGBoost, and Isolation Forest - utilizing metrics specifically designed for imbalanced datasets, such as Precision-Recall Area Under the Curve (PRAUC). Our results indicate that cost-sensitive XGBoost outperforms baseline models, achieving a PR-AUC of 0.868 and a minority-class recall of 0.857. To ensure model transparency, we employ SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) to interpret classification decisions, identifying specific latent behavioral patterns as the primary predictors of fraud. This research offers a robust, explainable methodology for financial institutions to improve fraud detection rates while maintaining high precision in highly imbalanced environments.  © 2026 IEEE.},
