	abstract = {In the wake of the dynamic cyber threats landscape, traditional security measures are failing to address sophisticated attacks such as zero-day exploits, advanced persistent threats (APTs), and polymorphic malware. Traditional security solutions rely on predefined signatures and static patterns that are not flexible enough to keep pace with new and unknown threats. As an emerging direction of improving cybersecurity, data mining techniques can unveil hidden patterns in large datasets. Data mining methods discussed here include classification, clustering, anomaly detection, and association rule mining as applied to real-world scenarios on cybersecurity challenges such as malware detection, fraud prevention, and insider threat detection. Additionally, with AI and ML being incorporated, it has also highly enhanced adaptability and efficiency of the models that data mining detects emerging threats. Among them are issues including a biased set of data, many false positives, and ever-changing cyber-attacks. New and emerging technologies are identified, which include federated learning, adversarial machine learning, and explainable AI, in order to scale up cybersecurity systems, to improve their robustness, and to increase the transparency of these systems. It underlines that there is an urgent need for intelligent and adaptive cybersecurity solutions for combating the threats from cyberworld that are increasingly moving ahead in rapid pace.  © 2025 IEEE.},
