	year = {2025},
	journal = {2025 ICCST - International Carnahan Conference on Security Technology, ICCST 2025},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/ICCST63435.2025.11295731},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031420523?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {CA, United States; OR, United States; WA, United States},
	abstract = {The retail industry is grappling with a rising fraud crisis because digital transformation has made it more straightforward for multi-faceted multi-channel threats to become prevalent. As fraudsters come up with new ways to cheat, traditional fraud detection systems that use separate machine learning models and strict rule-based engines become less effective. This position paper argues that Multi-Agent AI Systems (MAS) are now an effective means for identifying retail fraud since their algorithms are capable of developing and changing. Federated learning has been used by the framework in order to make sure that individuals can collaborate together securely, deep learning is used to identify complicated trends, and reinforcement learning is used to make the system more flexible. The system allows you to analyze information collected by different channels in real time, which reduces the amount of false positives and makes detection more accurate. The MAS architecture is more scalable and adaptable than previous models; however, it additionally presents challenges like complicated coordination, high implementation costs, and challenging governance. The paper discusses how to technically implement and integrate MAS through an organization. In addition, it discusses the trade-offs between system performance, operational efficiency, and following the rules. In order to utilize MAS to prevent retail fraud in the future, companies need to find a balance between being prepared to do business as usual and being innovative.  © 2025 IEEE.},
