	abstract = {Artificial intelligence (AI) is reshaping credit risk assessment in Supply Chain Finance (SCF) by moving from static, firm-level scoring to dynamic, ecosystem-aware evaluation. This paper presents a thematic review (153 studies screened, 42 retained) and proposes an integrated framework coupling the SCOR process model with a modular AI architecture. We synthesize key advances-supervised and unsupervised learning, deep temporal models, graph neural networks (GNNs), and NLP over alternative data-and map them to SCF use cases such as dynamic supplier scoring, liquidity forecasting, fraud detection, and ESG-linked financing. Beyond predictive accuracy, network-level modeling enhances resilience by revealing contagion pathways and stress points across multi-tier suppliers. We also highlight challenges-data silos, model opacity, organizational readiness, and regulatory constraints-and outline governance levers including explainable AI and cross-actor data sharing. Our contribution is twofold: (i) a consolidated view of AI techniques relevant for SCF credit risk; (ii) a conceptual bridge from predictive scoring to ecosystemic resilience, positioning AI as a strategic enabler of adaptive and equitable financing. © 2025 IEEE},
