	abstract = {Financial fraud detection in highly imbalanced datasets remains a critical challenge due to the rarity and evolving nature of fraudulent behavior. This study proposes a novel hybrid framework that combines unsupervised anomaly detection using One-Class SVM and cost sensitive learning with a deep neural network classifier to accurately identify fraudulent transactions. An engineered anomaly score is introduced as an additional feature to enhance separability in the latent space. Furthermore, a custom loss function is introduced to penalize false negatives more heavily than false positives. Evaluations are conduction on the 2013 European credit card dataset dataset, with performance evaluated through F1 Score, Area Under the Precision-Recall Curve (AUPRC), and Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (AUROC). The proposed model significantly outperforms classical machine learning models including Logistic Regression, SVM, Random Forest, k-Nearest Neighbors, and Naive Bayes. Notably, it achieves an AUROC of 0.9967 and an AUPRC of 0.8606. Moreover, explainability is addressed via surrogate decision trees to identify key contributing features. The results demonstrate that integrating anomalydriven features with deep learning improves both predictive accuracy and interpretability in fraud detection tasks. © 2025 IEEE.},
