	affiliations = {Liaocheng University, Liaocheng, China},
	abstract = {In the era of digital advancements, financial transactions have become increasingly complex, interconnected, and vulnerable to evolving frauds. Cases that severely damage the interests of investors are not uncommon, highlighting the urgent need for more reliable and transparent detection systems. Traditional models often struggle with the nonstationary nature of fraud patterns, high-dimensional data, and the inability to capture temporal dependencies in transaction sequences. This research proposes SwinDistill-XForest, a modular framework designed to improve fraud detection accuracy, generalization, and interpretability. The objective is to combine deep hierarchical feature extraction with efficient, regulator-compliant inference that supports real-time decision-making and transparent auditability. The SwinDistill-XForest methodology integrates Swin Transformer encoders for localized and contextual pattern learning, an Online Decoupled Knowledge Distillation (ODKD) strategy for multi-domain knowledge transfer, and a Random Forest student model enhanced with SHAP-based explainability. Numerical validation on IEEE-CIS and PaySim datasets shows that SwinDistill-XForest achieves 99.1% accuracy, 93.8% F1 score, and 99.7% Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (AUC-ROC) on IEEE-CIS, and 99.3% accuracy, 94.0% F1 score, and 99.8 % AUC-ROC on PaySim. These results confirm the SwinDistill-XForest's robustness, efficiency, and suitability for deployment in high-stakes financial environments.  © 2025 IEEE.},
