	abstract = {The emergence of GAN-based forgery tools has had a major negative impact on the integrity of digital documents, while the traditional human verification still shows an error rate of nearly 40%. In consequence, there have been great fraud losses in the financial, legal, and government sectors. This paper proposes a multi-modal deep learning authenticator that combines Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) for detecting forensic artifacts with Vision Transformers (ViTs) for global structural and semantic analysis by long-range dependency modeling. To move beyond binary verification, the multi-factor risk scoring engine in the system combines forensic features and AI-generation probabilities to assign documents to risk categories of low, medium, high, or uncertain, thus making the decision interpretable. The performance measures on benchmark and large-scale synthetic datasets show that the proposed method achieves 94.8% forgery detection accuracy, 92.1% AI-generated content detection accuracy, a 59% reduction in false positives, and a 50% decrease in processing latency enabling almost real-time deployment. The main point of this publication is the first risk-aware authentication framework that connects low-level texture forensics with high-level semantic analysis in one single decision pipeline. Next, the team will upgrade this framework with explainable AI and multimodal forensic capabilities to be able to fight against the real-world threats that emerge due to the increasingly sophisticated generative and adversarial media. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.},
	author_keywords = {AI-generated Content; Deep Learning Authenticator; Document Forgery Detection; Error Level Analysis; Image Forensics; Multi-modal Fusion; Risk Scoring; Vision Transformer},
