	abstract = {This research addresses the increasing threat of AI-generated deepfakes and telephone fraud by developing an AI-driven, explainable system to verify the authenticity of voice recordings. The proposed solution features a comprehensive processing pipeline incorporating data augmentation techniques, such as pitch shifting and adding Gaussian noise, to improve model generalisation across diverse acoustic environments. The study evaluates multiple architectures, specifically comparing the performance of Residual Networks against a hybrid model. Experimental results indicate that the ResNet architecture, when used with Mel-spectrogram features, achieves the highest effectiveness with an accuracy of 91.38% and an Equal Error Rate (EER) of 8.65%. A core contribution of this work is the integration of Explainable AI (XAI) modules, including Grad-CAM++ and Occlusion Sensitivity, which generate visual heatmaps to interpret model decisions. These methods allow for the identification of key decision-making features, such as speech structure or technical artefacts, transforming the “black box” model into a transparent tool. This approach improves user trust and provides an educational component to identify voice manipulation. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2027.},
