	affiliations = {Sree Vahini Institute of Science and Technology, CSE Department, Jntuk, A.P, Tiruvuru, India; Sree Vahini Institute of Science and Technology, Computer Science Department, A.P, India},
	abstract = {The paper examines the concept of explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) as a sophisticated paradigm of automated decision-making in financial frauds detection beyond standard traditional methods of interpretability that are not moving. The majority of available work involves using a model-agnostic approach to justify fraud classifications using SHAP or LIME, but these are unable to work when the methods of committing fraud change dynamically. The study investigates a two-step model. Phase 1 creates a hybrid detection system based on supervised classification and anomaly detection and transparent interpretability modules to hold the model accountable and conforming to regulatory frameworks. The goal of this phase is to describe model reasoning on both local and global levels and to build trust in the analyst and maintain the detection accuracy. Phase 2 builds upon this base by introducing adaptive explainability, in which explanatory models change along with the changing fraudulent behaviors. The paper identifies the importance of temporal drift detection, contextual modeling based on transformers and feedback-based refinement to maintain interpretive relevance over time. In contrast to previous work, this work includes a self-instructing explanation engine, which is able to modify its reasoning patterns as financial ecosystems evolve. The results of the study indicate that adaptive interpretability improves resilience, averts erosion of trust, and regulatory compliance in dynamic conditions of fraud. In general, the study provides an economical, open, and ever-changing fraud detection model, enhancing the accuracy of financial decisions with interpretation. © 2026 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Automated Code Generation; Exploratory Data Analysis; Instruction-Tuned Models; Multi-Language Software Development; Performance Evaluation; Transformer Architecture},
