	affiliations = {Mohan Babu University, (Erstwhile Sree Vidyanikethan Engineering College(Autonomous), Andhra Pradesh, Tirupati, India; Mohan Babu University, Department Of Computer Science And Engineering, A. P, Tirupati, India},
	abstract = {Online advertising has grown significantly, and in turn, we see a significant increase in click fraud. Bots that pass as humans to game the system with pay-per-click are a major issue we are seeing. The machine learning and deep learning models we use tend to be based on static data, which in turn makes realtime fraud detection a challenge. To that end, we present the Hybrid Adaptive Ensemble Framework (HAE), an effective solution we have put forth. It uses a mix of methods such as Light Gradient Boosting Machine (LightGBM), Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs), and Anomaly detection via Autoencoders. The model is a live learner that, from the input it receives, updates itself to the latest set of click data, which in turn keeps it up to date on the latest in fraud. Also, we see that the system identifies what is important in the data via transformer encoders. Then it improves on that with SHAP-based interpretability, which, in turn, provides the reason a given point is flagged as fraud. Also, the model uses SMOTE and handles concept drift well, which in turn takes care of imbalanced data and variable conditions in changing environments. What we have here is a scalable and very easy-to-understand HAE framework that also provides real-time fraud protection, thus making it a right solution for present-day digital advertising settings. © 2026 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Ad Click Fraud Detection; Explainable AI (XAI); Hybrid Adaptive Ensemble (HAE); Online Learning; Streaming Data Analytics},
