	abstract = {The quick developments in FinTech together with rising digital transaction speeds have made organizations need efficient scalable real-time fraud detection approaches to protect against fraudulent activities. The research describes PerfEvalFraudML as a predictive framework based on machine learning which specifically identifies fraud in real-time payment systems. The synthetic dataset featuring 20 representative transactions with transaction amount as well as velocity score and device type and transaction category and history of fraud variables underwent model training and testing with the RandomForest Classifier. Experimental findings indicated that the model demonstrated strong performance by reaching 92% accuracy with an F1-score of 0.89 and an AUC-ROC of 0.94 thus establishing an ideal sensitivity to precision ratio. Concerning model interpretation the eight visual performance analyses from the ROC family and confusion matrix methods delivered detailed understanding about how the model performed with multiple class groups. The established framework resolves key FinTech problems which include incorrect model detection alongside unbalanced database distribution as well as scalability issues and equitable treatment systems. The model executes training successfully within 1.2 seconds which makes it suitable for immediate real-time execution. The research presents recommendations toward future application of federated learning along with explainable AI tools to boost model interpretability. The presented research forms an important base to create trustworthy AI-powered fraud detection systems that excel in digital financial operations.  © 2025 IEEE.},
