	abstract = {Nowadays online recruitment platforms are commonly used to share the job opportunities. Their rapid growth increases the number of fake job postings online. These fake job postings harm the job seekers both financially and mentally. The traditional methods that rely on fixed rules or keyword matching are not very effective, as the scammers keep updating the way they write the job descriptions. This research proposes an explainable machine learning approach to detect fake job postings. The proposed framework combines textbased features using Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency (TF-IDF) with metadata from the job posts to increase the detection accuracy. The Synthetic Minority OverSampling Technique (SMOTE) is applied to deal with the extreme class imbalance in the dataset. The main detection model is an XGBoost classifier, which is compared with the Logistic Regression, Random Forest, LightGBM, and CatBoost baselines. Given the severe class imbalance in the dataset, F1score, Precision and Recall are adopted as the primary evaluation metrics. The study performs various experiments, including ablation results, stratified cross-validation, and McNemar's test to check statistical significance. To understand the decision-making process of the proposed model, SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) is used, which helps in understanding the results better. The proposed model achieves an F1-score of 0.7879 and an ROC-AUC score of 0.9634. According to the ablation study, prediction performance improves when the structural metadata is combined with the language patterns as compared to using text-only features. The findings emphasize the significance of explainable AI in fraud detection systems. © 2026 IEEE.},
