	affiliations = {VIT-AP University, School of Computer Science and Engineering, Amaravati, India},
	abstract = {The rise of real-time payment systems such as India's Unified Payments Interface (UPI) has transformed transaction behavior while introducing new security challenges. Al-though machine learning is widely used for fraud detection, most prior work studies adversarial robustness in experimental settings that do not reflect operational constraints of real payment systems. We propose a framework that incorporates UPI-specific limitations - fixed transaction attributes and rate caps - into adversarial robustness evaluation. Under realistic, constraint-aware attacks, we show that standard models (e.g., XGBoost) can be severely degraded (F1 falling from 0.71 to 0.04). SHAP-based analysis reveals how adversarial manipulations distort model reasoning. To defend against such attacks, we introduce UPI-Counterfactual Invariance Regularization (UPI-CIR), a training regularizer that enforces prediction invariance to constrained perturbations. Compared with conventional adversarial training, UPI-CIR substantially improves survivability under attack (F1 = 0.82) and also yields better performance on clean data. Our work establishes a more realistic standard for adversarial testing in financial systems and proposes a practical, domain-aware defense for securing modern payment infrastructures.  © 2025 IEEE.},
