	affiliations = {School of Systems Science and Industrial Engineering, State University of New York, Binghamton, United States},
	abstract = {Fraud detection in financial transactions presents significant challenges when working with highly imbalanced and heterogeneous data. This study proposes a novel deep learning framework that leverages minimal data preprocessing, automated hyperparameter tuning via Keras Tuner, and dual-level model explainability-local explanations using Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations (LIME) and global insights via Shapley Additive Explanations (SHAP), a unified approach based on Shapley values from cooperative game theory that quantifies the impact of each feature on the model's predictions-to achieve superior performance on the IEEE-CIS Fraud Detection dataset provided by Vesta Corporation, which contains a diverse mix of real-world e-commerce transactions. In contrast to many approaches that require extensive feature engineering, our method employs a streamlined pipeline that removes only those identity columns with over 90% missing values, fills residual missing data with the median, and applies label encoding only where necessary. The severe class imbalance is addressed by applying SMOTE, and a custom focal loss function is integrated into the neural network to focus learning on misclassified examples. Hyperparameters such as network depth, dropout rates, and learning rates are optimized using Keras Tuner's Hyperband algorithm. Detailed evaluations on a holdout validation set yield high ROC AUC, accuracy, precision, and recall. Additionally, due to the severe class imbalance, we report the Macro F1-score (81%) as a balanced metric that accounts for both precision and recall across classes. Local explainability is demonstrated through LIME visualizations (for true positives and false positives), while global feature importance is elucidated via SHAP. © 2025 IISE Annual Conference and Expo 2025, Conference Proceedings. All rights reserved.},
	author_keywords = {Class Imbalance; E-Commerce Transactions; Explainable AI; Fraud Detection; Machine Learning},
