	abstract = {Phishing websites remain a major cybersecurity threat, often leading to identity theft, financial fraud, and data breaches. Traditional defenses like blacklists and heuristic filters fail against zero-day attacks, where attackers modify URLs and site structures to avoid detection. This study proposes an explainable hybrid framework that integrates lexical and structural features from URLs and HTML with semantic representations derived from TF–IDF and DistilBERT embeddings. To handle dataset imbalance, a combined oversampling and cleaning strategy is applied, and classification is performed using CatBoost, a gradient-boosted decision tree model suited for mixed data types. Trained on the PhishStorm dataset and validated with the independent PhishTank Frontier dataset to mimic real-world zero-day scenarios, the framework achieved over 91% accuracy with a ROC–AUC of about 0.97. Explainability is ensured through SHAP and LIME, offering both global and local insights. A Python-based prototype using Flask and Streamlit demonstrates the model’s real-time phishing detection capability, confirming its potential for deployment in operational cybersecurity systems. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2027.},
