	abstract = {The purpose of this research is to develop and empirically validate an integrated framework for AGI-enabled neobanking that will minimize perceived financial, operational, and privacy risks through Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI), Blockchain-based security mechanisms, and Federated Learning mechanisms. Unlike earlier research on fintech concentrated on the isolated technology, this research suggests an integrated socio-technical governance model for a sustainable and ethical intelligent banking ecosystem. A hybrid framework, which is based on AGI-based decision intelligence, decentralized blockchain verification, privacy-preserving federated learning, and explainability modules, was designed. The mathematical expression combines risk prediction, trust estimation, and security validation in an optimized way. Experimental validation was done by structured financial data sets (50.000 transactions, 12.000 credit profiles, 8.500 fraud cases) and survey-based empirical analysis (n = 312 respondents) in order to measure perceived risk, trust, and behavioural intention. The proposed AGI framework was found to be 89.2% accurate in predicting credit risk, 90.1% in fraud detection, and 91.5% in transaction security evaluation, which is 5-12% better than the conventional machine learning models. Elucidation procedures assisted users in being more confident of AI systems by 14% than black box AI systems. The results of regression indicate that perceived risk is significantly negatively correlated with transparency (beta = 0.61, p < 0.01) and privacy assurance (beta = 0.57, p < 0.01). AGI, XAI, and blockchain integration can be significant in mitigating technological and behavioural risks and increasing compliance, governance and user confidence. © 2026, International Academic Institute for Science and Technology. All rights reserved.},
