	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105049042613?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This paper presents a comprehensive artificial intelligence framework for real-time risk assessment in digital payment systems, integrating supervised, unsupervised, and reinforcement learning paradigms. The architecture employs LSTM networks for sequential transaction modeling to detect temporal fraud patterns, autoencoders for unsupervised anomaly identification in mobile wallet behaviors, and hybrid XGBoost-transformer models for dynamic risk scoring of cross-border payments. These components operate within a streaming data pipeline (Apache Kafka/Flink) enabling sub-two-second latency at scale. Evaluated on synthetic and real-world financial datasets, the system achieves 90-96% detection accuracy with 30-50% reduction in false positives compared to traditional rule-based methods. The framework incorporates explainability tools (SHAP/LIME) for regulatory compliance, federated learning capabilities for privacy-preserving cross-institutional collaboration, and agentic AI mechanisms for autonomous adaptation to emerging fraud patterns. Three validated use cases - credit card fraud detection, mobile wallet anomaly detection (Apple Pay/Google Pay/African platforms), and cross-border payment risk scoring - demonstrate the framework's applicability across diverse financial contexts. This work provides financial institutions with an integrated blueprint for balancing security, user experience, and regulatory compliance in modern payment infrastructures. © 2026 IEEE.},
